WEBVTT

1
00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:55.052
<i>The '50s were</i> a <i>grand time,
if you were</i> a <i>man.</i>

2
00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:59.220
<i>I'm Dick Nolan. I make things up
for</i> a <i>living. I'm a reporter.</i>

3
00:02:12.399 --> 00:02:15.573
<i>It's the strangest goddamn story
that I ever covered.</i>

4
00:02:15.736 --> 00:02:19.240
<i>It started the day that Margaret Ulbrich
walked out on her suffocating husband,</i>

5
00:02:19.406 --> 00:02:22.512
<i>long before it became
the fashionable thing to do.</i>

6
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.321
Come on, Janie.

7
00:04:10.584 --> 00:04:13.428
Sugar! You made it!

8
00:04:13.587 --> 00:04:16.591
- You're in North Beach!
- Look at you, Deirdre!

9
00:04:16.757 --> 00:04:18.361
- Dee-Ann.
- Oh! Dee-Ann.

10
00:04:18.525 --> 00:04:21.125
I know, but I came on the scene
and Deirdre sounded like something

11
00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:24.140
my mother would call me.
So are you flipping for all this?

12
00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:26.868
- Are you settled? How's Jane?
- Jane is swell.

13
00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.706
She's going to
this sweet, little school.

14
00:04:28.869 --> 00:04:31.229
You know, it's really hard
without her father, and I just...

15
00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:32.578
- ...don't know if we're gonna be...
- Stop that.

16
00:04:32.739 --> 00:04:34.377
- What?
- You're better off.

17
00:04:34.541 --> 00:04:36.543
Between us, I never liked Frank.

18
00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.553
- You were a bridesmaid.
- Exactly.

19
00:04:38.712 --> 00:04:41.784
That's why I couldn't say anything.
But if I see you wrong off again,

20
00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:44.792
I will tell you. Now, come on,
let's have some fun.

21
00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:48.592
For jazz, check out the Hungry I.
For Italian, Vanessi's.

22
00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:52.669
For salvation, try the Buddhist temple.
For an, Six Gallery.

23
00:04:52.826 --> 00:04:55.466
- Do they only show Modern?
- Everyone only shows Modern.

24
00:04:55.629 --> 00:04:57.302
And in the basement,
they have espresso.

25
00:04:57.464 --> 00:04:59.239
Espresso, what's that?
Is that like reefer?

26
00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:01.139
You have a lot to learn.

27
00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:14.288
<i>Back then,
women didn't leave.</i>

28
00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:16.688
<i>Not without</i> a <i>job or prospects.</i>

29
00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:19.091
<i>All she had was her paintings
in the trunk,</i>

30
00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:21.289
<i>daughter in the backseat.</i>

31
00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:23.024
Good luck.

32
00:05:28.829 --> 00:05:30.035
<i>OKAY-</i>

33
00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:36.846
We don't get many ladies in here.

34
00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:38.846
So, your husband approves
of you working?

35
00:05:39.206 --> 00:05:41.846
Uh, my husband and I are separated.

36
00:05:43.543 --> 00:05:46.717
Separated? Hm...

37
00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:52.357
I realize that I have
no employment experience,

38
00:05:52.619 --> 00:05:57.068
but I really do need this job.
I have a daughter to support.

39
00:05:57.891 --> 00:06:00.872
I'm not very good at tooting my
own horn, but I do love to paint.

40
00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:03.564
So if I could just
show you my portfolio.

41
00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:08.338
Um, I studied at the
Watkins Art Institute in Nashville,

42
00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:10.845
then I took some illustration classes
in New York.

43
00:06:11.004 --> 00:06:14.349
This is a... it's a pastel that I did.

44
00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:20.185
And, uh, this is a charcoal portrait.

45
00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:23.857
You do understand this
is a furniture company?

46
00:06:59.286 --> 00:07:02.859
Okay, sounds great.

47
00:07:03.023 --> 00:07:06.129
- You see anything you like?
- I like those over there.

48
00:07:06.293 --> 00:07:08.739
- They're really neat.
- Yeah.

49
00:07:08.895 --> 00:07:11.705
- Your stuff is cute.
- Thank you.

50
00:07:11.865 --> 00:07:15.074
- How much?
- Today's special, $2.

51
00:07:15.235 --> 00:07:16.771
I'll give you $1.

52
00:07:18.305 --> 00:07:20.751
- Fill right.
- All right, go ahead, Dylan.

53
00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:22.580
Go ahead and have a seat.

54
00:07:23.076 --> 00:07:25.249
- What's your name?
- My name is Dylan.

55
00:07:25.412 --> 00:07:28.416
- Dylan. And how old are you?
- I'm nine years old.

56
00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:31.084
Nine years old, wow.

57
00:07:34.754 --> 00:07:38.133
- It's like a Monet.
- Monet?

58
00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:42.262
Monet?
That's a hell of a compliment.

59
00:07:42.429 --> 00:07:45.342
Though, if I may
respectfully disagree,

60
00:07:45.498 --> 00:07:47.739
I'm more in the tradition of Pissarro.

61
00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:53.008
Come, come a little closer.
Come on. Good!

62
00:07:53.173 --> 00:07:57.588
See these daring specks of bold color'?

63
00:07:57.744 --> 00:08:00.918
Do you wanna touch it?
Come on.

64
00:08:01.081 --> 00:08:05.962
Go on, do it. I don't lay it on thick.
You're not gonna break it.

65
00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:09.764
H's $35.

66
00:08:11.124 --> 00:08:13.434
Would you excuse me one second?

67
00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.501
Here we are.

68
00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:22.170
- Here you go.
- Thank you.

69
00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:24.110
Oh, thank you.

70
00:08:27.607 --> 00:08:31.646
You're better than spare change.
You shouldn't sell yourself so cheap.

71
00:08:31.811 --> 00:08:36.521
- Well, I'm just glad they liked it.
- Oh! You're past that point.

72
00:08:36.683 --> 00:08:39.459
Your heart's in your work.

73
00:08:41.021 --> 00:08:42.227
What's your name?

74
00:08:44.524 --> 00:08:46.401
Margaret.

75
00:08:47.193 --> 00:08:50.197
Look, wouldn't you rather, uh, flirt
with those girls over there?

76
00:08:50.363 --> 00:08:52.809
No. I like you, Margaret...

77
00:08:53.833 --> 00:08:55.574
Ulbrich.

78
00:08:55.735 --> 00:09:00.206
You know, Margaret Ulbrich,
you undervalue yourself.

79
00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.214
Let me show you how it's done.

80
00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:05.812
Little girl!

81
00:09:05.979 --> 00:09:09.051
How would you like
your portrait sketched

82
00:09:09.215 --> 00:09:13.322
by the world-renowned
Margaret Ulbrich, Queen of the Bay?

83
00:09:13.486 --> 00:09:16.433
In mere minutes,
she captures your soul!

84
00:09:16.589 --> 00:09:18.569
- Nah.
- “Nah”?

85
00:09:18.725 --> 00:09:21.331
Don't you wish this were you
in this beautiful painting?

86
00:09:21.494 --> 00:09:22.734
But that is me.

87
00:09:22.896 --> 00:09:25.900
And that's me,
and that one started as me,

88
00:09:26.066 --> 00:09:28.569
then Mother turned it
into a Chinese boy.

89
00:09:28.735 --> 00:09:33.741
Hm... My apologies.
I misconstrued the situation.

90
00:09:33.907 --> 00:09:35.614
Well, I'd better mosey along

91
00:09:35.775 --> 00:09:39.018
before Mr. Ulbrich comes back
and socks me in the eye.

92
00:09:41.714 --> 00:09:44.957
Mr. Ulbrich is out of the picture.

93
00:09:51.424 --> 00:09:54.268
- Bonsoir, Henri.
- Oh, Monsieur Keane!

94
00:09:54.461 --> 00:09:56.532
Sorry for not calling first.

95
00:09:56.696 --> 00:10:00.974
Monsieur, your regular table is ready.
Madame, bonsoir Bienvenue.

96
00:10:01.201 --> 00:10:03.613
Thank you.

97
00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.147
And I don't have to pay.

98
00:10:05.305 --> 00:10:07.376
I'm set because
I gave the chef a painting.

99
00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:09.110
- You know what he said?
- What?

100
00:10:09.275 --> 00:10:13.587
He said, "No one paints Montmartre
like Walter Keane!"

101
00:10:15.815 --> 00:10:19.558
I can't believe
you lived in Paris.

102
00:10:19.919 --> 00:10:23.890
Best time of my life.

103
00:10:25.725 --> 00:10:27.830
I've never even been on an airplane.

104
00:10:27.994 --> 00:10:32.272
Oh, you have to experience these things.
You have to grab them.

105
00:10:32.432 --> 00:10:35.003
I wanted to be an artist,
so I just went!

106
00:10:35.168 --> 00:10:37.512
Studied painting at the Beaux-Arts.

107
00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:41.447
Lived in a Left Bank studio.
Survived on bread and wine.

108
00:10:41.608 --> 00:10:44.589
- You are a romantic.
- Damn right you are.

109
00:10:46.246 --> 00:10:49.955
Of course, walking away
from the bourgeois scene wasn't easy.

110
00:10:50.116 --> 00:10:54.690
I had to quit my job... leave my wife.

111
00:10:57.157 --> 00:11:00.195
Yeah, these choices aren't easy.

112
00:11:01.661 --> 00:11:03.800
I've never acted freely.

113
00:11:03.997 --> 00:11:07.672
I was a daughter, and then a wife,
and then a mother.

114
00:11:09.169 --> 00:11:12.446
All of my paintings are of Jane
because she's all I know.

115
00:11:12.605 --> 00:11:16.951
Don't knock your work.
You have an amazing talent.

116
00:11:17.143 --> 00:11:20.386
You can look at someone
and capture them on canvas.

117
00:11:20.547 --> 00:11:22.788
You can paint people.

118
00:11:22.949 --> 00:11:24.986
I can only paint things.

119
00:11:25.151 --> 00:11:29.190
- Yeah, my street scenes are...
- charming,

120
00:11:29.355 --> 00:11:34.236
but at the end of the day, they're just
a collection of buildings and sidewalks.

121
00:11:35.128 --> 00:11:38.632
Walter, I bet you can paint anything.

122
00:11:41.501 --> 00:11:45.313
When you look at me like that,
I could fall hard.

123
00:11:51.744 --> 00:11:55.715
I'm sorry.
This is just moving really fast.

124
00:11:58.184 --> 00:12:01.188
I haven't been on a date in a long time.

125
00:12:12.565 --> 00:12:14.670
Jane, sweetie, stop fidgeting.

126
00:12:14.834 --> 00:12:18.782
Mother, after all this time,
you must know what my face looks like.

127
00:12:22.542 --> 00:12:24.749
Hey. Your canvas is blank.

128
00:12:24.911 --> 00:12:28.051
- You can't rush inspiration.
- Jane.

129
00:12:28.214 --> 00:12:31.354
Don't bother Mr. Keane.
You know creativity comes from within.

130
00:12:31.517 --> 00:12:34.930
Don't worry. She's not bothering me.

131
00:12:44.931 --> 00:12:47.104
I gotta ask you a question.

132
00:12:47.267 --> 00:12:51.113
- What's that with the big crazy eyes?
- Oh.

133
00:12:51.271 --> 00:12:56.220
Well, I believe that you can see things
in the eyes.

134
00:12:56.376 --> 00:12:59.152
The eyes are the window to the soul.

135
00:12:59.312 --> 00:13:02.486
Yeah, but...
you paint them like pancakes.

136
00:13:02.649 --> 00:13:05.095
They're way out of proportion.

137
00:13:05.251 --> 00:13:08.960
Well, eyes are
how I express my emotions.

138
00:13:09.122 --> 00:13:12.126
I've always drawn them like that.

139
00:13:12.292 --> 00:13:14.932
When I was little, I had surgery
that left me deaf for a period,

140
00:13:15.128 --> 00:13:18.837
and I couldn't hear,
so I found myself staring.

141
00:13:18.998 --> 00:13:21.478
I relied on people's eyes.

142
00:13:21.634 --> 00:13:24.410
Walter?  Hey, Walt!

143
00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:26.572
- I thought that was you!
- Hi, Don.

144
00:13:26.773 --> 00:13:28.184
BOY, am I glad to see you!

145
00:13:28.341 --> 00:13:30.651
Hey, have we heard back
from the city on that setback?

146
00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.188
My guys really need the variance
for the first ﬂoor retail.

147
00:13:33.346 --> 00:13:36.190
- We'll hear from Permits by Thursday.
- Yeah? That's terrif!

148
00:13:36.349 --> 00:13:39.990
I'll tell the architects. Okay.

149
00:13:43.623 --> 00:13:45.034
What was that?

150
00:13:51.497 --> 00:13:53.340
I didn't want you to know.

151
00:13:55.034 --> 00:13:57.640
I'm in commercial real estate.

152
00:13:57.804 --> 00:14:01.616
- You're a realtor?
- Yeah.

153
00:14:01.774 --> 00:14:04.778
A hugely successful realtor.

154
00:14:04.944 --> 00:14:08.050
Top earner in the downtown office
three years running.

155
00:14:08.214 --> 00:14:11.127
And you're ashamed?

156
00:14:11.317 --> 00:14:14.958
Well, any blockhead
can arrange a sublet.

157
00:14:16.489 --> 00:14:20.494
All I ever wanted was
to support myself as an artist.

158
00:14:21.694 --> 00:14:26.143
I tried to make a clean break,
but couldn't cut it.

159
00:14:27.734 --> 00:14:30.977
I'm just a Sunday painter.

160
00:14:34.741 --> 00:14:39.315
You're such
a gentleman carrying it all the way.

161
00:14:43.249 --> 00:14:45.752
Thank you. Um, just come right in here.

162
00:14:48.488 --> 00:14:50.695
Just set it down over here.
I just like to...

163
00:14:50.857 --> 00:14:52.617
- Right here?
- Yeah. That's great. I just...

164
00:14:52.759 --> 00:14:55.763
like to keep it out of the way.

165
00:14:55.928 --> 00:15:01.537
This is... one that I'm working on.

166
00:15:01.701 --> 00:15:05.274
You can see that...
I'm working on it.

167
00:15:30.096 --> 00:15:31.598
I got the mail.

168
00:15:33.599 --> 00:15:34.873
The mail...

169
00:15:49.649 --> 00:15:51.754
What's wrong?

170
00:15:52.118 --> 00:15:56.123
Oh... Um...
Frank wants to take Jane away.

171
00:15:56.289 --> 00:15:59.463
- He says that I'm an unfit mother...
- You're a perfect mother.

172
00:15:59.625 --> 00:16:01.104
Hm...

173
00:16:02.495 --> 00:16:06.671
He told the courts that, um,
she lacks a proper home

174
00:16:06.833 --> 00:16:11.339
and that it is beyond my capabilities
as a single mother...

175
00:16:11.504 --> 00:16:13.142
Marry me.

176
00:16:17.743 --> 00:16:20.417
- Walter! I...
- Shh.

177
00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:22.082
Don't think of a reason to say no,

178
00:16:22.248 --> 00:16:26.287
'cause I've got a million reasons
to say yes.

179
00:16:26.452 --> 00:16:29.262
- I know it makes no sense.
- No, it doesn't...

180
00:16:29.422 --> 00:16:32.266
But just think
of the fun we'll have!

181
00:16:32.425 --> 00:16:35.463
And I'm going to take care of you girls.

182
00:16:38.698 --> 00:16:42.111
- Oh!
- Margaret, I'm on my knee!

183
00:16:42.268 --> 00:16:44.976
Come on, what do you say?
Let's get married.

184
00:16:45.137 --> 00:16:49.847
- We could be in Hawaii by the weekend.
- Oh, Hawaii? Marriage?

185
00:16:50.943 --> 00:16:52.583
I don't know, Walter,
I'm crazy about you.

186
00:16:52.678 --> 00:16:55.784
I'm just really overwhelmed right now.
Why would we go to Hawaii? Why?

187
00:16:55.948 --> 00:17:00.863
Because you're a princess and you
deserve to get married in paradise.

188
00:17:20.306 --> 00:17:24.755
You were right!
It's paradise!

189
00:17:24.911 --> 00:17:28.723
- Only God could create these colors.
- I knew you'd love it.

190
00:17:30.516 --> 00:17:35.590
- Can't we just stay here forever?
- Well, I don't know about forever.

191
00:17:35.755 --> 00:17:38.929
But I guess I could arrange
for another week.

192
00:18:17.129 --> 00:18:20.599
That's that waterfall, and
the air was so fresh you could taste it.

193
00:18:20.766 --> 00:18:22.939
This was, um, an ancient altar.

194
00:18:23.102 --> 00:18:27.278
That's a statue of Kane,
the god of creation.

195
00:18:27.607 --> 00:18:30.213
- Hm!
- I said a prayer to him.

196
00:18:30.376 --> 00:18:33.653
Oh, and that's Walter and Janie,
they built that sandcastle together.

197
00:18:34.981 --> 00:18:37.325
Wow, this is all happening mighty quick.

198
00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:40.622
In the time you moved here, I've had
two dates and you're already married.

199
00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:44.563
Well, I thought there was a void
in my life and Walter filled it.

200
00:18:44.724 --> 00:18:46.169
Walter's filled a lot of things.

201
00:18:46.325 --> 00:18:48.464
He's diddled every skirt
on the an circuit.

202
00:18:48.628 --> 00:18:50.429
That is my husband
that you are talking about.

203
00:18:50.496 --> 00:18:53.966
I know, that's why I brought it up.

204
00:18:54.133 --> 00:18:57.080
- I'm not naive.
- Hm...

205
00:18:57.236 --> 00:19:00.615
I am naive.
But I know who I married.

206
00:19:00.773 --> 00:19:02.810
Walter can be rash,
but he's a good provider.

207
00:19:02.975 --> 00:19:05.478
And he's really wonderful with Jane.

208
00:19:05.678 --> 00:19:09.490
We're just looking to make a fresh
start. I am a divorcee with a child.

209
00:19:09.682 --> 00:19:11.923
Walter is a blessing.

210
00:19:13.919 --> 00:19:15.091
Thanks.

211
00:19:28.534 --> 00:19:33.176
"You are on the threshold
of untold success.“

212
00:19:33.439 --> 00:19:36.147
What's brilliant about the composition
is its spontaneity.

213
00:19:36.308 --> 00:19:38.219
The image has no visual center
of attention.

214
00:19:38.377 --> 00:19:40.880
- It's quite gestural.
- Oh, definitely.

215
00:19:41.047 --> 00:19:42.617
Strongly influenced by the Tachistes.

216
00:19:43.549 --> 00:19:46.393
I heard Tab Hunter
was in here looking at one.

217
00:19:46.619 --> 00:19:49.361
Well, I'm not allowed to say, but...

218
00:19:55.461 --> 00:19:57.498
Oh, Christ.

219
00:19:57.663 --> 00:20:00.200
Don't come in here, not now.
Please.

220
00:20:00.366 --> 00:20:03.904
Ruben! Good day!
Do you got a minute?

221
00:20:04.136 --> 00:20:06.810
Walter, in polite society,
the word is "appointment."

222
00:20:06.972 --> 00:20:08.645
Um, I could some back later.

223
00:20:09.842 --> 00:20:12.516
You're gonna
love my stuff today.

224
00:20:16.682 --> 00:20:18.184
Haven't I seen that one before?

225
00:20:19.685 --> 00:20:23.155
No! That was painted
in the 5th Arrondissement.

226
00:20:23.322 --> 00:20:25.768
This is the 6th Arrondissement.

227
00:20:25.925 --> 00:20:27.885
I don't understand.
You lived in Paris for a week.

228
00:20:28.027 --> 00:20:29.506
How can you still
be cranking out paintings?

229
00:20:29.662 --> 00:20:33.041
It's all up here. And here.

230
00:20:33.199 --> 00:20:34.542
Well, ifs not going up here.

231
00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:37.408
Walter, you know we don't go
for that representational jazz.

232
00:20:37.570 --> 00:20:38.947
You're too literal.

233
00:20:39.105 --> 00:20:41.085
- Art isn't fashion.
- Yes, it is.

234
00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:46.883
People want Kandinsky or Rothko.
They don't want goopy street scenes.

235
00:20:50.015 --> 00:20:51.050
How about this?

236
00:21:02.194 --> 00:21:04.196
Good God! You've entered a new period.

237
00:21:05.898 --> 00:21:09.209
No. They're my wife's.

238
00:21:12.037 --> 00:21:14.381
Why are their eyes so big?

239
00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:18.078
- Like big, stale jellybeans.
- It's Expressionism.

240
00:21:18.244 --> 00:21:20.588
Surely, you recognize it.

241
00:21:22.047 --> 00:21:25.085
Well, I'm just glad
you two found each other.

242
00:21:25.584 --> 00:21:26.995
So what do you say?

243
00:21:27.219 --> 00:21:30.063
- I say no. it's not art.
- "It's not art“?

244
00:21:30.256 --> 00:21:32.258
It's like the back of a magazine.
You know, "Draw the turtle!

245
00:21:32.458 --> 00:21:34.062
Send in a nickel,
win the big contest!"

246
00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:36.171
How dare you!
Lots of people will like this.

247
00:21:36.328 --> 00:21:38.774
Well, nobody who's walking
through the doors of this gallery.

248
00:21:38.964 --> 00:21:42.935
Now, please, clear out the clutter
before the taste police arrive.

249
00:21:59.852 --> 00:22:01.695
We'll never break in!

250
00:22:02.321 --> 00:22:05.165
There's a secret society
of gallery owners and critics

251
00:22:05.324 --> 00:22:09.363
who get together for Sunday brunch
in Sausalito deciding what's "cool."

252
00:22:09.528 --> 00:22:11.872
Like McCarthy, in his hearings:

253
00:22:12.097 --> 00:22:17.103
"I anoint that painter.
I banish that painter to Nowheresville."

254
00:22:18.137 --> 00:22:22.051
I think people buy art
because it touches them.

255
00:22:22.274 --> 00:22:25.050
Yeah, you're living in fairyland.

256
00:22:25.211 --> 00:22:27.987
People don't get to discover anything.

257
00:22:28.147 --> 00:22:31.492
They buy an because it's
in the right place at the right time.

258
00:22:34.153 --> 00:22:37.032
Okay, give it up for Cal Tjader!

259
00:22:37.189 --> 00:22:40.796
<i>That set was humming!</i>

260
00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:44.703
All right, be sure to stick around
for the 1 a.m. show!

261
00:22:48.801 --> 00:22:54.649
Oh, Yeah. Enjoy yourself.
Enjoy. See you again.

262
00:22:54.807 --> 00:22:56.150
Excuse me.

263
00:22:56.308 --> 00:22:59.915
Hey, Banducci. I love the music tonight.
It's a gas.

264
00:23:00.079 --> 00:23:04.755
- Oh, thanks. Thank you.
- Walter Keane. I'm a painter.

265
00:23:04.984 --> 00:23:08.932
I was looking at your walls.
They're pretty plain-.

266
00:23:09.088 --> 00:23:12.763
Oh. Well, maybe you're right.
What color were you thinking?

267
00:23:12.925 --> 00:23:16.839
No, no, I'm an artist.
I used to be based on the Left Bank.

268
00:23:16.996 --> 00:23:22.070
Now I've relocated to the States
and I'm looking for, um...

269
00:23:22.234 --> 00:23:24.407
an exhibition venue.

270
00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:26.243
I like my club the way it is.

271
00:23:26.505 --> 00:23:29.418
Your stuff is so hot,
go put it in a museum.

272
00:23:29.575 --> 00:23:34.046
Okay.  I respect that.
You're a businessman, not a charity.

273
00:23:34.213 --> 00:23:39.686
How about if I rented your walls?

274
00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:03.943
Walter, Margaret, we're ready.

275
00:24:16.388 --> 00:24:20.803
Ah, an art lover! Beautiful!
What can I do for you, sir?

276
00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:22.837
I'm just looking for the john.

277
00:24:24.797 --> 00:24:26.105
Thanks.

278
00:24:28.567 --> 00:24:32.447
I think you're going
to have to ask him.

279
00:24:32.604 --> 00:24:34.413
Honestly, I think you...
How did you find him?

280
00:24:34.573 --> 00:24:37.019
Oh, it was easy...

281
00:24:56.662 --> 00:24:59.370
No!

282
00:25:02.034 --> 00:25:03.707
Look at that child.

283
00:25:04.803 --> 00:25:06.942
She's so sad.

284
00:25:07.973 --> 00:25:11.511
- Is she poor'?
- She's forgotten.

285
00:25:11.677 --> 00:25:13.816
It just makes me want to cry.

286
00:25:17.182 --> 00:25:20.527
- Are you Keane?
- Yes, I am.

287
00:25:20.686 --> 00:25:23.166
Well, you're a hell of a painter.

288
00:25:23.322 --> 00:25:26.235
Thank you. Thank you very much.

289
00:25:26.392 --> 00:25:28.394
Your work is very powerful.

290
00:25:29.695 --> 00:25:34.075
There's so much emotion in those eyes.
Mm!

291
00:25:35.567 --> 00:25:39.140
- Is something wrong?
- No.

292
00:25:39.304 --> 00:25:42.683
- I didn't realize you meant that waif.
- Oh, I get it.

293
00:25:42.841 --> 00:25:47.017
The artist doesn't wanna part
with his favorite piece.

294
00:25:47.179 --> 00:25:49.921
Oh, sweetie, thank you! I love it!

295
00:25:52.551 --> 00:25:55.225
I'll be quick.

296
00:26:04.263 --> 00:26:06.573
Ladies.

297
00:26:06.732 --> 00:26:10.043
Hey, Picasso! Nice crowd, huh?

298
00:26:10.202 --> 00:26:13.206
You wouldn't know it
from that broom closet you parked me in.

299
00:26:13.372 --> 00:26:15.943
What are you talking about?
That's prime thoroughfare.

300
00:26:16.108 --> 00:26:17.849
People drink,
they gotta relieve themselves.

301
00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:20.957
That's insulting! When people see art,
they shouldn't think of shit!

302
00:26:21.113 --> 00:26:24.890
Hey! Watch it with the purple language.
Hey, there's ladies present here!

303
00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:26.393
Hey!

304
00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:34.030
<i>'</i> Oh!

305
00:26:41.033 --> 00:26:43.138
I've never posted bail before.

306
00:26:43.302 --> 00:26:46.613
I'm sorry. Banducci laughed at our art,
so I soaked him.

307
00:26:46.772 --> 00:26:49.378
Since when are you thin-skinned?
Artists have to handle criticism.

308
00:26:49.541 --> 00:26:53.318
I know. You're right.
But I was in a bad place already.

309
00:26:53.479 --> 00:26:57.427
I'd had a couple, then I made some guy
believe I painted your big eyes.

310
00:26:57.583 --> 00:27:01.497
What? I don't understand.
Why would you do such a thing?

311
00:27:01.653 --> 00:27:05.931
It was a misunderstanding. And then,
I didn't want to jinx the sale.

312
00:27:07.993 --> 00:27:10.667
Well, don't ever do it again.

313
00:27:19.304 --> 00:27:20.942
- Hey!
- Don't give me a hard time.

314
00:27:21.106 --> 00:27:22.483
I'm just grabbing my stuff.

315
00:27:27.746 --> 00:27:31.250
Can you believe it? Were sold out!
I don't even have a headliner!

316
00:27:31.416 --> 00:27:33.327
Hell, it's a Monday!

317
00:27:33.485 --> 00:27:36.796
Walter, we made the front page.

318
00:27:36.989 --> 00:27:39.697
People are here because
they wanna see the sappy paintings

319
00:27:39.958 --> 00:27:42.336
that made grown men fight.

320
00:27:43.929 --> 00:27:46.773
- I'm sick of you, Keane!
- I'll see you in court,

321
00:27:46.965 --> 00:27:49.126
- you son of a bitch!
- You're gonna see me, all right.

322
00:27:49.168 --> 00:27:52.877
- I'll sue you for assault!
- I'm sick of you and your paintings!

323
00:27:53.105 --> 00:27:56.678
I'll... I'll sue you for slander!
And false arrest!

324
00:27:56.842 --> 00:27:59.789
- You son of a bitch!
- Let's go see.

325
00:28:09.955 --> 00:28:12.492
<i>Walter Keane
was not</i> a <i>subtle man.</i>

326
00:28:12.658 --> 00:28:15.036
<i>But subtle doesn't sell.</i>

327
00:28:17.129 --> 00:28:18.506
Yes, sir.

328
00:28:18.664 --> 00:28:22.305
That was quite a load of horseshit
you gents were laying out there.

329
00:28:22.467 --> 00:28:24.071
Dick Nolan. The Examiner.

330
00:28:25.504 --> 00:28:29.884
Hey, pal, don't lose any sleep.
I eat this stuff up with a spoon.

331
00:28:30.042 --> 00:28:31.919
Gives me something to write about
in my column.

332
00:28:32.077 --> 00:28:35.058
I thought you were only
doing celebrities.

333
00:28:35.214 --> 00:28:37.751
Well, Banducci's famous
and you hit him.

334
00:28:37.916 --> 00:28:41.659
So you're a celebrity, once removed.
Buy me a drink.

335
00:28:44.022 --> 00:28:47.265
Hey, Gary. I'll have a Ward Eight
in a frosted highboy.

336
00:28:47.426 --> 00:28:49.497
And my friend will have the same.

337
00:28:53.365 --> 00:28:57.336
So, Walter,
tell me about your work.

338
00:28:59.071 --> 00:29:02.109
- When I was in Paris...
- Oh, no, Jesus, not those.

339
00:29:03.909 --> 00:29:06.219
I mean the little hobo kids.

340
00:29:22.261 --> 00:29:25.731
- Mm...
- Wake up!

341
00:29:25.897 --> 00:29:27.968
Wake up!

342
00:29:28.133 --> 00:29:32.047
- Mm... Hi.
- You were a hit!

343
00:29:32.237 --> 00:29:36.310
What a night!
I sold out all your big eyes!

344
00:29:36.475 --> 00:29:39.922
- There must be $200!
- They adore you!

345
00:29:40.112 --> 00:29:41.750
- Walter!
- Because of that article,

346
00:29:41.947 --> 00:29:44.325
the joint was packed!

347
00:29:44.483 --> 00:29:49.557
And then a famous journalist showed up,
and... I need more paintings!

348
00:29:49.755 --> 00:29:51.598
- Walter!
- Now!

349
00:29:51.757 --> 00:29:55.671
That's gonna take at least a week!
I need to do sketching and painting...

350
00:29:58.230 --> 00:30:00.972
Of course! But this is an opportunity!

351
00:30:01.133 --> 00:30:03.977
- Yeah.
- We're gonna make a crackerjack team.

352
00:30:04.136 --> 00:30:08.278
Me schmoozing up the clubs, while you're
back here, doing what you love!

353
00:30:19.951 --> 00:30:22.192
Get out of there!

354
00:30:28.660 --> 00:30:30.503
I love you.

355
00:31:33.558 --> 00:31:35.902
The eyes are so powerful.

356
00:31:36.061 --> 00:31:37.904
You know, a poet once said

357
00:31:38.063 --> 00:31:41.272
the eyes are the windows of the soul.

358
00:31:41.433 --> 00:31:44.243
That's why I paint them so big.
I've always done it that way.

359
00:31:44.403 --> 00:31:48.613
If you're interested in that style,
I'm working on a few new pieces.

360
00:31:48.774 --> 00:31:54.383
I have a little blond girl in a yellow
dress that'll tear your heart out.

361
00:31:54.579 --> 00:31:56.217
Walter?

362
00:31:56.381 --> 00:31:58.418
Baby! What are you doing here?

363
00:32:00.185 --> 00:32:01.858
Why are you lying?

364
00:32:03.121 --> 00:32:05.032
Excuse me.

365
00:32:06.792 --> 00:32:08.965
You were taking credit for something
that isn't yours.

366
00:32:09.127 --> 00:32:11.903
No. I was just trying to close the deal.

367
00:32:12.063 --> 00:32:13.804
These children are a part of my being.

368
00:32:14.032 --> 00:32:17.309
I'm a salesman. You know buyers
pay more if they meet the painter.

369
00:32:17.469 --> 00:32:20.575
They couldn't meet me
because you told me to stay home.

370
00:32:20.772 --> 00:32:24.310
Look, we're making money. Your pocket,
my pocket'? Where's the difference?

371
00:32:24.476 --> 00:32:27.252
- You're taking this so lightly.
- Not at all!

372
00:32:27.412 --> 00:32:30.120
This is not about ego.
You wanna say you did the street scenes?

373
00:32:30.282 --> 00:32:32.592
Fine. I don't care.
Say a monkey painted them.

374
00:32:33.452 --> 00:32:35.227
Well, I'm glad that you
can just dash away your work

375
00:32:35.387 --> 00:32:37.492
without any emotional connection,
but...

376
00:32:37.656 --> 00:32:42.401
Oh, honey, I just want to share them
with the world.

377
00:32:42.561 --> 00:32:45.064
Would you rather have your children
piled in a closet,

378
00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:47.471
or hanging in someone's living room?

379
00:32:55.173 --> 00:32:56.413
Who's that man'?

380
00:32:56.575 --> 00:32:59.351
That is Dino Olivetti,
of Olivetti typewriters.

381
00:32:59.511 --> 00:33:02.515
But don't even think about it, Walter.
He doesn't speak a lick of English.

382
00:33:02.681 --> 00:33:05.457
Hey! Welcome!

383
00:33:05.617 --> 00:33:09.690
Good to see you, good to see you.

384
00:33:30.709 --> 00:33:34.156
Um, Mr. Olivetti is enchanted
by the painting.

385
00:33:34.312 --> 00:33:36.849
He would like to know
who is the artist?

386
00:33:53.965 --> 00:33:55.842
- I am!

387
00:33:56.001 --> 00:34:00.643
It's a delight to meet you, Signore!

388
00:34:00.805 --> 00:34:03.046
Have you been
an art lover for long?

389
00:34:03.208 --> 00:34:05.552
I call this piece...

390
00:34:28.567 --> 00:34:30.672
Five grand!

391
00:34:30.835 --> 00:34:34.009
We made $5,000!

392
00:34:34.172 --> 00:34:36.709
And it wasn't even
for one of your good ones!

393
00:34:36.875 --> 00:34:40.721
- Don't you mean one of your good ones?
- No. No, no! No.

394
00:34:40.879 --> 00:34:43.450
All right, "our."
One of our good ones.

395
00:34:43.615 --> 00:34:47.961
We're now hanging in the collection
of Italian industrialist Dino Olivetti.

396
00:34:48.119 --> 00:34:53.467
With his patronage comes credibility,
and with credibility comes respect.

397
00:34:53.625 --> 00:34:55.969
- Well, what about honesty?
- Oh, come on.

398
00:34:56.127 --> 00:35:01.099
The painting says "Keane."
I'm Keane, you're Keane.

399
00:35:01.266 --> 00:35:04.406
From now on, we're one and the same.

400
00:35:04.569 --> 00:35:08.107
Mm...

401
00:35:12.277 --> 00:35:17.590
<i>My sources tell me that the mayor
is to expect</i> a <i>big surprise today.</i>

402
00:35:17.749 --> 00:35:20.286
On behalf of the children of the world,

403
00:35:20.452 --> 00:35:24.662
I present this painting
to the mayor of San Francisco!

404
00:35:27.258 --> 00:35:28.698
My man on the street <i>tells</i> me

405
00:35:28.793 --> 00:35:31.637
<i>that the Soviet ambassadors
in town this</i> week.

406
00:35:32.397 --> 00:35:34.502
In the interest of peace
through culture,

407
00:35:34.666 --> 00:35:38.443
I donate this painting to the people
of the Soviet Union.

408
00:35:40.505 --> 00:35:45.853
The Purple Onion. 9:30.
Joan Crawford has a dinner reservation.

409
00:35:48.747 --> 00:35:52.354
Miss Crawford, in recognition
of your cinematic craft,

410
00:35:52.517 --> 00:35:55.020
we bestow this painting on you.

411
00:35:58.690 --> 00:36:00.294
<i>The cat is out of the bag.</i>

412
00:36:00.458 --> 00:36:03.803
<i>I can reveal that our local luminary,
Walter Keane,</i>

413
00:36:03.962 --> 00:36:06.169
<i>is opening his own gallery.</i>

414
00:36:11.369 --> 00:36:13.508
Ruben's gonna choke when he sees this.

415
00:36:16.041 --> 00:36:18.021
I remember when Mama painted that.

416
00:36:23.882 --> 00:36:26.886
Are you sure?
That would have been a while ago.

417
00:36:27.052 --> 00:36:29.362
Sure I'm sure.
It was in our old apartment,

418
00:36:29.521 --> 00:36:31.125
and you had me sit on the stool
in the kitchen.

419
00:36:31.289 --> 00:36:35.203
No, sweetie, you're confused.
I painted that.

420
00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:38.068
No, Mother did.
I'm wearing my blue dress.

421
00:36:39.564 --> 00:36:41.840
Lots of girls have that dress.

422
00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:44.310
You have a good eye.

423
00:36:44.469 --> 00:36:48.178
I painted it, but I tried
to mimic your mother's style.

424
00:36:48.339 --> 00:36:51.252
You know,
the style she used to paint in.

425
00:36:53.344 --> 00:36:55.017
Well, you did a really good job.

426
00:37:13.364 --> 00:37:14.934
Hello.

427
00:37:15.100 --> 00:37:19.048
I'm sorry' I've never done this before,
so um, don't know...

428
00:37:19.838 --> 00:37:22.682
I was raised Methodist,
so if that's a problem, I can go.

429
00:37:22.841 --> 00:37:26.288
No, no, please.
We don't chase people away.

430
00:37:26.511 --> 00:37:30.288
- Okay.
- What is troubling you?

431
00:37:31.349 --> 00:37:36.264
Well, um... I lied to my child.

432
00:37:37.055 --> 00:37:39.535
Why would you do that?

433
00:37:39.691 --> 00:37:42.797
My husband,
he pressured me to do it.

434
00:37:43.027 --> 00:37:46.099
I've just never lied to her before.
I'm not that kind of person.

435
00:37:46.297 --> 00:37:48.277
Is your husband that kind of person?

436
00:37:50.468 --> 00:37:53.472
Um, I don't think of him that way.

437
00:37:53.638 --> 00:37:56.881
I mean, he likes to tell stories.
Maybe he exaggerates a bit,

438
00:37:57.041 --> 00:37:59.715
but, um, he's a good man,

439
00:37:59.878 --> 00:38:03.291
and he's just trying to save enough
money to buy a house for the family.

440
00:38:03.448 --> 00:38:07.897
But what of the child?
Will this lie bring harm to her?

441
00:38:08.052 --> 00:38:12.558
Harm? No, no. No. I don't...

442
00:38:13.992 --> 00:38:16.666
I'm just looking for some answers.

443
00:38:18.062 --> 00:38:20.975
Well, the modern world
is a complicated place.

444
00:38:21.166 --> 00:38:23.908
- Mm-hm.
- Occasionally, children may need

445
00:38:24.068 --> 00:38:26.674
to be sheltered from certain truths.

446
00:38:26.838 --> 00:38:29.842
No. No, it's not like...

447
00:38:30.008 --> 00:38:31.487
It sounds as if your husband

448
00:38:31.643 --> 00:38:35.022
is trying to make the best
of an imperfect situation.

449
00:38:35.246 --> 00:38:38.921
You were raised Christian,
you know what we are taught:

450
00:38:39.083 --> 00:38:41.427
the man is head of the household.

451
00:38:41.586 --> 00:38:45.432
Perhaps you should...
trust his judgment.

452
00:38:53.531 --> 00:38:56.375
I think it's creepy,
maudlin and amateurish.

453
00:38:56.534 --> 00:38:59.845
Exactly. I love it.

454
00:39:05.210 --> 00:39:08.487
- We got in early. We own three.
- Oh.

455
00:39:10.615 --> 00:39:12.322
Thanks.

456
00:39:12.483 --> 00:39:15.794
Hey...

457
00:39:17.555 --> 00:39:21.799
Hey, baby.
Killer party. It's a happening.

458
00:39:21.960 --> 00:39:24.031
So where's your stuff?

459
00:39:24.195 --> 00:39:27.904
Oh, uh, we decided that this
would just be Walter's show.

460
00:39:28.066 --> 00:39:31.479
Oh, “we" did?
Why would "we" do that?

461
00:39:31.636 --> 00:39:34.480
Because Walter is more established.

462
00:39:34.639 --> 00:39:38.212
It's strange, you know,
Walter doesn't really strike me

463
00:39:38.409 --> 00:39:40.685
as the cute, hungry kitten type.

464
00:39:42.647 --> 00:39:46.823
It's really good to see you.
Thank you for coming.

465
00:39:52.423 --> 00:39:55.495
Your husband's quite a talent.

466
00:39:56.394 --> 00:39:58.533
Do you paint, too?

467
00:40:00.265 --> 00:40:04.577
Uh, I don't know.

468
00:40:13.077 --> 00:40:16.581
I'm curious about your technique.
How long did that piece take to execute?

469
00:40:16.781 --> 00:40:20.354
That'? Mm. Probably months.

470
00:40:20.551 --> 00:40:22.963
You know, first the thinking,
the sketching,

471
00:40:23.121 --> 00:40:26.261
then the time with just me and the oils.

472
00:40:26.424 --> 00:40:29.098
Oils? But isn't that acrylic?

473
00:40:29.260 --> 00:40:31.763
Oh, that! Yeah.

474
00:40:32.030 --> 00:40:35.739
Yeah, it's like a jumble of ideas

475
00:40:35.967 --> 00:40:38.447
rattling around in my brain.

476
00:40:40.305 --> 00:40:42.410
So where do you get your ideas?

477
00:40:44.943 --> 00:40:47.253
What do you mean?

478
00:40:47.412 --> 00:40:52.122
I mean... why are they
all images of children?

479
00:40:54.652 --> 00:40:57.064
<i>May I remind you, Miss Rogers,
that you're still under oath.</i>

480
00:41:00.124 --> 00:41:01.967
<i>Miss Rogers,</i>

481
00:41:02.126 --> 00:41:04.299
<i>you testified that</i>

482
00:41:04.462 --> 00:41:06.408
<i>when you met Judson Bailey
in Core/H's,</i>

483
00:41:06.564 --> 00:41:08.840
<i>you?! been talking to a man a! the bar.</i>

484
00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:11.742
<i>- Well, I mm understand.
- Couldn't he have been some stranger</i>

485
00:41:11.903 --> 00:41:14.611
<i>who just struck up a conversation
with Mr. Bailey?</i>

486
00:41:14.806 --> 00:41:18.117
- No!
- Thank you, Mrs. Stone. That?! be ah'.

487
00:41:18.276 --> 00:41:20.916
Perry Mason
<i>will</i> return after this.

488
00:41:21.679 --> 00:41:23.920
New York Times
art <i>critic</i> John Canaday

489
00:41:24.082 --> 00:41:26.494
<i>with a perspective
into the work of Walter Keane.</i>

490
00:41:27.352 --> 00:41:30.526
<i>Keane's work is completely
without distinction.</i>

491
00:41:30.755 --> 00:41:33.668
<i>He is not a member
of the Society of Western Artists.</i>

492
00:41:33.825 --> 00:41:35.099
<i>He has won no awards.</i>

493
00:41:35.293 --> 00:41:40.800
<i>He's only noteworthy for his appearances
In</i> a <i>certain newspaper's gossip columns.</i>

494
00:41:40.965 --> 00:41:44.344
<i>Mr, Keane is why society
needs critics</i>

495
00:41:44.535 --> 00:41:48.005
<i>to protect them from such atrocities.</i>

496
00:41:48.172 --> 00:41:50.311
Mom!

497
00:41:50.475 --> 00:41:51.954
Mom!

498
00:41:52.143 --> 00:41:54.180
Can I come in'?

499
00:41:54.345 --> 00:41:58.816
Uh... no, you can't.
Mama's busy right now.

500
00:42:00.985 --> 00:42:02.362
What are you doing in there?

501
00:42:11.362 --> 00:42:13.364
Janie, sweetie, come.

502
00:42:13.531 --> 00:42:16.808
You need to respect
your mother's privacy.

503
00:42:16.968 --> 00:42:21.747
Sometimes grownups
need alone time. Come.

504
00:42:29.247 --> 00:42:32.023
Is that the ice cream truck'?

505
00:42:36.754 --> 00:42:39.360
Go get yourself a Fudgsicle.

506
00:42:47.165 --> 00:42:51.409
Ooh! Out of this world!

507
00:42:51.669 --> 00:42:54.673
I don't know.
I'm not comfortable with this.

508
00:42:54.839 --> 00:42:57.217
Jane and I used to be really close,
and now...

509
00:42:57.375 --> 00:43:00.379
Eh, Jane's fine.
She's eating ice cream.

510
00:43:00.578 --> 00:43:04.526
She has new shoes.
She even has a college fund.

511
00:43:04.682 --> 00:43:08.755
Maybe... I'm just lighthearted
from all this turpentine.

512
00:43:08.920 --> 00:43:12.390
- I've been up here all day.
- I need your help.

513
00:43:12.557 --> 00:43:15.936
I want to go on television
to defend our an.

514
00:43:17.261 --> 00:43:21.038
- Wait, you're going on television?
- Yeah, but what am I gonna say?

515
00:43:21.232 --> 00:43:26.875
Meaning, what compels me
to paint these paintings?

516
00:43:28.539 --> 00:43:30.985
Maybe you have an unhealthy obsession
with little girls.

517
00:43:31.142 --> 00:43:34.487
- Cute.
- I'm sorry.

518
00:43:34.712 --> 00:43:37.454
Looks like you've painted yourself
into a corner.

519
00:43:37.648 --> 00:43:40.322
You want heat this winter'?

520
00:43:40.485 --> 00:43:41.828
Help me out.

521
00:43:42.053 --> 00:43:43.999
Walter, art is personal.

522
00:43:48.292 --> 00:43:51.603
What compels a grown man
to paint a picture like this?

523
00:43:51.796 --> 00:43:56.472
Because I... I grew up
surrounded by six sisters?

524
00:43:56.634 --> 00:43:59.740
I grew up in an orphanage?

525
00:43:59.904 --> 00:44:04.148
I lived in a world
where adults had vanished

526
00:44:04.308 --> 00:44:07.482
and kids and kittens ran wild

527
00:44:07.645 --> 00:44:10.091
over a desolate landscape?

528
00:44:11.516 --> 00:44:13.962
What about your street scenes?
Why did you paint those?

529
00:44:14.118 --> 00:44:17.964
Well, I lived it. I experienced it.

530
00:44:18.122 --> 00:44:20.864
Oh, was it all sunlit-dappled streets
and flower vendors?

531
00:44:21.025 --> 00:44:24.438
Of course not. It was after the war.

532
00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:32.442
There was destruction everywhere.

533
00:44:34.338 --> 00:44:38.343
I traveled the continent.

534
00:44:38.543 --> 00:44:41.046
The ravages were horrifying.

535
00:44:41.212 --> 00:44:44.785
<i>My psyche was scarred
in my an student days.</i>

536
00:44:44.982 --> 00:44:49.453
<i>Nothing in my fife
has ever made such an impact</i>

537
00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:51.998
as the sight of the children.

538
00:44:52.190 --> 00:44:55.069
The war-wracked innocents,

539
00:44:55.293 --> 00:44:57.398
without parents, without homes,

540
00:44:57.562 --> 00:45:00.406
fighting over garbage.

541
00:45:00.631 --> 00:45:03.407
That's where my life

542
00:45:03.568 --> 00:45:07.141
as a painter began in earnest.

543
00:45:09.407 --> 00:45:15.221
From then on, I painted the lost children
with the big eyes.

544
00:45:16.814 --> 00:45:21.263
Those eyes that forever will retain
their haunting quality.

545
00:45:32.330 --> 00:45:36.107
Mr. Keane!

546
00:45:48.779 --> 00:45:52.226
Thank you so much.

547
00:45:52.383 --> 00:45:54.385
Thank you.

548
00:45:56.787 --> 00:45:58.357
Mr. Keane.

549
00:46:00.791 --> 00:46:05.035
- How many sales today?
- Sales? None with this crowd.

550
00:46:05.196 --> 00:46:08.769
These people are all looky-loos.
They can't afford these paintings.

551
00:46:08.933 --> 00:46:11.072
But we have been giving away
a lot of posters.

552
00:46:20.745 --> 00:46:22.952
It's the craziest thing.

553
00:46:23.114 --> 00:46:26.152
I started charging for the posters.

554
00:46:26.317 --> 00:46:28.729
First a nickel, then a dime.

555
00:46:28.886 --> 00:46:31.958
Yes, Maggie! It's cuckoo!

556
00:46:32.790 --> 00:46:34.269
But then it got me thinking.

557
00:46:34.425 --> 00:46:36.996
Would you rather
sell one $500 painting

558
00:46:37.161 --> 00:46:39.767
or a million cheaply reproduced posters?

559
00:46:39.930 --> 00:46:44.606
See, folks don't care if it's a copy.
They just want art that touches them.

560
00:46:44.769 --> 00:46:47.477
Then we could sell it anywhere.

561
00:46:47.638 --> 00:46:49.413
Everywhere!

562
00:48:34.412 --> 00:48:36.858
It stinks in here.
You should open a window.

563
00:48:37.014 --> 00:48:40.291
- What time is it?
- 6:30.

564
00:48:40.451 --> 00:48:42.260
What do you got back there?
Let me see.

565
00:48:42.420 --> 00:48:43.922
Oh, Walter, it's personal.

566
00:48:44.088 --> 00:48:45.931
Personal? We're married.

567
00:48:46.090 --> 00:48:47.865
We're not supposed to have secrets
from each other.

568
00:48:48.025 --> 00:48:50.972
Come on, put it up there.
Put it up there.

569
00:49:00.438 --> 00:49:02.440
It's a completely different style.

570
00:49:03.574 --> 00:49:04.814
Yes, it is.

571
00:49:04.975 --> 00:49:06.977
It kind of looks like you.

572
00:49:07.144 --> 00:49:10.591
Well, ifs a self-portrait.

573
00:49:14.885 --> 00:49:16.728
How am I gonna explain that?

574
00:49:16.887 --> 00:49:19.231
I was thinking that...

575
00:49:21.258 --> 00:49:23.761
maybe I could sign it myself.

576
00:49:23.928 --> 00:49:27.068
That sounds a bit confusing,
doesn't it'?

577
00:49:27.231 --> 00:49:28.767
"Keane" means me.

578
00:49:28.933 --> 00:49:31.812
I know, but when people ask me
if I paint,

579
00:49:31.969 --> 00:49:33.846
I don't know how to answer
and it would be nice

580
00:49:34.004 --> 00:49:36.450
to have the pride of being able to say,
"That's mine."

581
00:49:36.607 --> 00:49:39.417
- Who'd you tell about the big eyes?
- Nobody.

582
00:49:39.577 --> 00:49:43.320
If you tell anyone,
this empire collapses!

583
00:49:43.481 --> 00:49:47.156
Do you wanna give back the money?
We've committed fraud!

584
00:49:47.318 --> 00:49:49.525
I know!

585
00:49:49.787 --> 00:49:53.758
My God, I know! I live with this
every minute of my life!

586
00:49:53.958 --> 00:49:57.132
I've kept my end of the bargain.

587
00:49:57.294 --> 00:50:00.969
I have not told anyone.

588
00:50:01.765 --> 00:50:06.009
Please. Just let me have this.

589
00:50:06.604 --> 00:50:08.641
I didn't know you painted, Margaret.

590
00:50:08.806 --> 00:50:10.945
Yeah, we don't talk about it.

591
00:50:12.042 --> 00:50:14.545
Sadly, people don't buy lady art.

592
00:50:14.712 --> 00:50:16.350
What about Georgia O'Keeffe?

593
00:50:19.650 --> 00:50:21.994
So, Margaret,
where do you get your ideas?

594
00:50:22.887 --> 00:50:26.562
Oh, um, well, from the world around me.

595
00:50:26.790 --> 00:50:30.203
And I just love Modigliani's

596
00:50:30.361 --> 00:50:31.499
- use of line.
- Modi-what?

597
00:50:31.662 --> 00:50:33.972
- Modigliani.
- For Christ's sake, Margaret.

598
00:50:34.131 --> 00:50:36.907
Dick writes a gossip column.
Stick to the family angle.

599
00:50:37.167 --> 00:50:39.306
Get a gander at little Janie over here.

600
00:50:39.503 --> 00:50:43.645
What a talent!
Look at these Keanes.

601
00:50:43.874 --> 00:50:46.354
When you cut open our veins,
we bleed oil.

602
00:50:54.385 --> 00:50:55.989
Ah! A little treat!

603
00:50:57.021 --> 00:51:00.594
The fourth member
of the Painting Keanes.

604
00:51:00.758 --> 00:51:03.705
Lily, sweetie, how are you?

605
00:51:03.861 --> 00:51:06.000
- I'm fine, Dad.
- Good.

606
00:51:06.163 --> 00:51:08.939
- I lost a tooth.
- Really?

607
00:51:09.099 --> 00:51:10.908
Did you get in a fight?

608
00:51:11.068 --> 00:51:12.570
No, it fell out.

609
00:51:12.736 --> 00:51:16.684
Well, is the tooth fairy something
I gotta deal with,

610
00:51:16.840 --> 00:51:18.717
or did your mom already handle it?

611
00:51:18.876 --> 00:51:20.219
She handled it.

612
00:51:20.377 --> 00:51:23.620
Mm... Good! Good, good!

613
00:51:23.781 --> 00:51:27.854
Janie, come.
Come, show her the kids' room.

614
00:51:28.018 --> 00:51:33.092
And then you girls can join the fun.

615
00:51:37.194 --> 00:51:41.142
Walter, you never told me
you had another daughter.

616
00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:46.211
Didn't I? Sure.
Lily's from my first marriage.

617
00:51:46.370 --> 00:51:47.610
Oh.

618
00:51:50.608 --> 00:51:51.985
Excuse me.

619
00:51:52.142 --> 00:51:53.883
I'm sorry, excuse me.

620
00:51:56.046 --> 00:51:59.550
Walter?
May I have a word with you'?

621
00:52:03.654 --> 00:52:05.998
- What is going on in there?
- That's Lily.

622
00:52:06.156 --> 00:52:10.002
- I'm sure I mentioned her.
- No, you didn't.

623
00:52:11.061 --> 00:52:13.507
- Is she moving in?
- No!

624
00:52:13.664 --> 00:52:15.735
Her mother's going to Vegas
for the weekend.

625
00:52:15.899 --> 00:52:18.859
I'm supposed to have her once a month,
but I don't make her mom enforce it.

626
00:52:18.936 --> 00:52:20.847
How could you lie
about something so big?

627
00:52:21.005 --> 00:52:23.417
- Oh, she's a very sweet girl.
- I'm sure she is.

628
00:52:23.574 --> 00:52:27.021
Well, I put up with your daughter
and never said a peep.

629
00:52:27.578 --> 00:52:30.149
I'm gonna pretend you
didn't say that. Okay'?

630
00:52:30.314 --> 00:52:32.692
I'm sorry. Sorry.

631
00:52:32.850 --> 00:52:35.592
Please, let's just get through this.

632
00:52:41.325 --> 00:52:43.498
Any connoisseur
of fine an can tell you,

633
00:52:43.661 --> 00:52:47.507
Walter Keane and Paul Gauguin
have a lot in common.

634
00:52:47.665 --> 00:52:51.340
Both walked away from a career
to travel the globe.

635
00:52:51.502 --> 00:52:52.776
Frankly, I'm...

636
00:52:52.936 --> 00:52:55.041
<i>I've known a lot
of famous personalities,</i>

637
00:52:55.205 --> 00:52:58.618
<i>and only</i> a <i>certain type of creature
can live in that spotlight.</i>

638
00:52:58.776 --> 00:52:59.811
<i>Waiter had the chops.</i>

639
00:52:59.977 --> 00:53:03.015
<i>Margaret? Not so much.</i>

640
00:53:05.716 --> 00:53:07.889
- I painted that.
- Really?

641
00:53:08.052 --> 00:53:09.463
Hm...

642
00:53:09.620 --> 00:53:12.191
It's very... evocative.

643
00:53:14.124 --> 00:53:16.126
Sensual.

644
00:53:17.828 --> 00:53:19.000
Oh.

645
00:53:21.231 --> 00:53:22.471
"M D H"?

646
00:53:24.835 --> 00:53:28.180
You're so... mysterioso.

647
00:53:29.640 --> 00:53:32.416
Yes. We don't use my name,

648
00:53:32.576 --> 00:53:35.648
since people don't
take women's art seriously.

649
00:53:35.813 --> 00:53:38.225
“MDH" are my initials.

650
00:53:38.382 --> 00:53:41.522
And more.
I'm really interested in numerology,

651
00:53:41.685 --> 00:53:44.325
and, as you know,
seven is a very good number.

652
00:53:44.488 --> 00:53:47.332
- Seven?
- Mm. Luckily, my maiden name

653
00:53:47.491 --> 00:53:48.663
is Margaret Doris Hawkins.

654
00:53:48.826 --> 00:53:50.999
"M" is the 13th letter
of the alphabet,

655
00:53:51.161 --> 00:53:52.731
"D" is four, "H" eight.

656
00:53:52.896 --> 00:53:54.933
And if you add up one
and three of 13...

657
00:53:55.099 --> 00:54:00.412
an orgy of color and sun...
sunshine. Excuse me.

658
00:54:00.571 --> 00:54:04.678
Which makes
four plus four plus eight equals 16,

659
00:54:04.842 --> 00:54:06.788
and then one plus six equals seven.

660
00:54:06.944 --> 00:54:10.221
- Maggie? One second.
- Mm-hm.

661
00:54:12.216 --> 00:54:15.857
Good grief! What are you babbling about?
Long division?

662
00:54:16.019 --> 00:54:18.693
Could you please help the world
and shut your mouth?

663
00:54:18.856 --> 00:54:22.201
The only number you want in his head
is the price.

664
00:54:22.459 --> 00:54:25.770
Two nuts that fell from the same tree.
It's insufferable.

665
00:54:25.929 --> 00:54:27.738
Why are we starving
while they print money?

666
00:54:27.898 --> 00:54:30.708
Because that nut's a genius.
He sells paintings.

667
00:54:30.868 --> 00:54:32.279
Then he sells pictures of the paintings.

668
00:54:32.436 --> 00:54:35.110
Then he sells postcards of pictures
of the paintings.

669
00:54:35.272 --> 00:54:37.718
I'll bet I could bang one out
in ten minutes.

670
00:54:37.875 --> 00:54:41.982
- It wouldn't have the dopey sincerity.
- Ah, the customers won't notice.

671
00:54:47.584 --> 00:54:49.564
Christ. It's a movement.

672
00:55:19.183 --> 00:55:20.924
Oh. Dee-Ann!

673
00:55:21.084 --> 00:55:23.621
My God! I thought
I had the wrong address.

674
00:55:23.787 --> 00:55:26.529
I know,
it's a really long driveway.

675
00:55:27.524 --> 00:55:29.834
Honestly, I can't believe I live here.

676
00:55:29.993 --> 00:55:32.166
- Whoa.
- I know.

677
00:55:32.329 --> 00:55:37.039
We've got two acres,
a pool, five bedrooms.

678
00:55:37.201 --> 00:55:39.374
Though I thought that was excessive,

679
00:55:39.536 --> 00:55:43.279
since there's only three of us here.

680
00:55:43.473 --> 00:55:46.010
It's been so long since I've seen you.

681
00:55:46.310 --> 00:55:47.948
I know.

682
00:55:48.111 --> 00:55:50.648
North Beach is 30 miles,
might as well be 300.

683
00:55:50.814 --> 00:55:54.660
Well, you're probably busy hanging out
with all your new rich buddies.

684
00:55:54.818 --> 00:55:58.527
Please. Those are Walter's friends.
He brings people by.

685
00:55:58.689 --> 00:56:02.501
- The Beach Boys came by.
- Wow.

686
00:56:04.461 --> 00:56:08.739
- Are you hungry?
- I'm thirsty.

687
00:56:08.899 --> 00:56:12.176
When we moved in, I thought that
a wet bar was kind of extravagant,

688
00:56:12.336 --> 00:56:16.045
but you'd be surprised
how much use you can get out of it.

689
00:56:16.206 --> 00:56:21.713
- So how is Walter?
- Oh. Well, he couldn't be happier.

690
00:56:21.879 --> 00:56:23.440
He has everything he's ever dreamed of.

691
00:56:23.547 --> 00:56:26.858
And so do you. Fabulous.

692
00:56:30.854 --> 00:56:32.663
Is that your art studio?

693
00:56:32.823 --> 00:56:35.667
Oh. No, you don't wanna go in there.

694
00:56:35.826 --> 00:56:37.100
I wanna take a peek.

695
00:56:37.261 --> 00:56:40.936
I wanna see what the workspace of
a wildly successful artist looks like.

696
00:56:41.098 --> 00:56:43.100
No, no, no. No.

697
00:56:43.267 --> 00:56:45.406
Dee-Ann, please do not go down there.

698
00:56:45.569 --> 00:56:49.676
Look... Look, you can't go in there.

699
00:56:54.211 --> 00:56:56.248
Walter paints here, too.

700
00:56:58.115 --> 00:56:59.560
Is Walter home now'?

701
00:57:01.118 --> 00:57:05.089
- What the hell's going on?
- Oh. Nothing. Um...

702
00:57:06.290 --> 00:57:10.067
I just... invited Dee-Ann over
for some...

703
00:57:11.128 --> 00:57:15.042
You know I don't like anyone
seeing my work before it's done!

704
00:57:34.651 --> 00:57:38.224
You and your whole
non-representational crowd are frauds!

705
00:57:38.388 --> 00:57:40.629
Shut up!
You're so full of shit, Walter!

706
00:57:40.791 --> 00:57:43.328
Get outta my house!
Get out of my big house!

707
00:57:43.493 --> 00:57:47.964
Go back to selling your coat hanger
sculptures in Fisherman's Wharf!

708
00:57:48.131 --> 00:57:50.236
Fuck you!

709
00:57:55.138 --> 00:57:57.641
I don't want her ever
invited here again.

710
00:57:58.308 --> 00:57:59.912
I won't.

711
00:58:04.848 --> 00:58:07.351
- Oh, you gonna get that ball?
- Good boy. Oh!

712
00:58:07.517 --> 00:58:11.693
Oh! Good boy.
All right, Janie, I'm gonna get to work.

713
00:58:11.855 --> 00:58:14.995
Can I come?
No, I can never come.

714
00:58:15.158 --> 00:58:17.468
No, I shouldn't even ask.

715
00:58:33.877 --> 00:58:35.652
Now, how did you get in here? Hm?

716
00:58:35.812 --> 00:58:40.158
Didn't you hear'?
There are no visitors.

717
00:58:41.885 --> 00:58:44.024
This is what it's come to, huh?

718
00:58:44.187 --> 00:58:47.896
You are the only living soul
I can tell my secret to.

719
00:58:48.058 --> 00:58:52.871
Well... I painted them.

720
00:58:53.030 --> 00:58:55.704
I did. Every single one of them.

721
00:58:55.866 --> 00:58:58.710
Every big eye. Me.

722
00:58:59.536 --> 00:59:01.538
And no one will ever know but you.

723
00:59:03.473 --> 00:59:05.885
And I'm gonna paint
another one right now.

724
00:59:06.043 --> 00:59:11.254
To lick Walter off, maybe I'll paint one
about a crazy girl talking to a dog.

725
01:00:24.087 --> 01:00:28.160
I had a productive day today.

726
01:00:29.593 --> 01:00:32.301
Stumblers! onto some ho'! gossip.

727
01:00:32.462 --> 01:00:37.411
Madame Chiang Kai-shek is coming
to town, straight from Taipei.

728
01:00:38.335 --> 01:00:41.782
I think we should present her
with a painting.

729
01:00:41.938 --> 01:00:44.976
Get Dick to flack it.

730
01:00:45.142 --> 01:00:48.954
Or the hell with Dick.
I met a new guy at UPI.

731
01:00:49.112 --> 01:00:52.787
Maybe, uh... she'd like one
of your street scenes.

732
01:00:52.949 --> 01:00:57.420
Yeah, I don't know. I thought you could
whip off a doodle of Chinatown.

733
01:00:57.587 --> 01:01:01.660
With a cute little girl,
sort of a big-eyed, slanty-eyed thing.

734
01:01:02.993 --> 01:01:04.666
No.

735
01:01:04.828 --> 01:01:08.674
No, no. She is a dignitary,
and she deserves a piece

736
01:01:08.832 --> 01:01:10.505
that comes straight from you.

737
01:01:12.669 --> 01:01:14.444
- You think?
- Uh-huh.

738
01:01:14.604 --> 01:01:15.947
Maybe you're right.

739
01:01:16.106 --> 01:01:20.384
She probably doesn't have a Parisian
street scene hanging in her palace.

740
01:01:20.544 --> 01:01:23.957
Unless Madame Chiang Kai-shek
already has a Cenic.

741
01:01:26.816 --> 01:01:28.625
- What's that?
- A Cenic.

742
01:01:28.785 --> 01:01:32.665
That's the painter that painted
all your early work.

743
01:01:39.796 --> 01:01:41.469
It's Scenic!

744
01:01:41.631 --> 01:01:43.907
That's my nickname in Paris!

745
01:01:44.067 --> 01:01:47.207
All my art school pals
loved my scenic views so much,

746
01:01:47.370 --> 01:01:49.730
they called me... well,
they couldn't pronounce it properly,

747
01:01:49.739 --> 01:01:52.686
so they called me "Cenic."

748
01:01:52.876 --> 01:01:55.720
The more you lie, the smaller you seem.

749
01:01:56.880 --> 01:02:00.919
How dare you accuse me of lying!
I'm proud of my early Cenics!

750
01:02:01.084 --> 01:02:02.586
Then why did you paint over the name?

751
01:02:02.752 --> 01:02:05.733
A piece of advice,
never use water-based over an oil.

752
01:02:05.889 --> 01:02:07.425
It'll just ﬂeck right off.

753
01:02:08.692 --> 01:02:13.232
You sound crazy!
For God's sake, you've seen me paint!

754
01:02:14.931 --> 01:02:16.740
No, I haven't.

755
01:02:18.835 --> 01:02:22.681
I always thought that I had,
but it's like a mirage.

756
01:02:22.839 --> 01:02:26.446
From a distance,
you look like a painter,

757
01:02:26.710 --> 01:02:29.384
but up close there's
just not much there.

758
01:02:31.448 --> 01:02:35.191
I've been to Paris!
I studied at the Beaux-Arts!

759
01:02:35.352 --> 01:02:37.525
At the Grand Chaumiere!

760
01:02:37.687 --> 01:02:41.191
I spent hours and hours in the Louvre,

761
01:02:41.358 --> 01:02:43.269
staring at the greatness
of the masters!

762
01:02:43.426 --> 01:02:45.099
Walter...

763
01:02:46.863 --> 01:02:49.434
have you even been to Paris?

764
01:03:00.443 --> 01:03:04.914
I wanted...
I so wanted to become an artist.

765
01:03:05.081 --> 01:03:07.994
It just never turned out so well.

766
01:03:19.763 --> 01:03:22.141
I don't want you
sleeping here any longer.

767
01:03:22.299 --> 01:03:26.441
There are three extra bedrooms
in this house. Go pick one.

768
01:03:47.524 --> 01:03:49.333
How many more of these
are in the back?

769
01:03:49.492 --> 01:03:52.803
- I don't know. Um, maybe 3,000 or so.
- Does the printer owe us more?

770
01:03:52.962 --> 01:03:55.533
Excuse me, miss.
How much is this painting?

771
01:03:55.699 --> 01:03:59.169
- Go! What do I pay you for'?
- Yes, Mr. Keane.

772
01:03:59.336 --> 01:04:00.644
Sorry about that...

773
01:04:04.307 --> 01:04:06.514
It's not even one of mine.
It's one of hers.

774
01:04:16.786 --> 01:04:18.732
This doesn't change anything.

775
01:04:20.623 --> 01:04:23.866
- I know the truth.
- Who cares?

776
01:04:24.027 --> 01:04:26.507
Maybe it's time to shake things up.

777
01:04:26.663 --> 01:04:30.372
- Start putting my name on the MDHs.
- No!

778
01:04:30.533 --> 01:04:33.912
Absolutely not! I already hate myself
for giving you the waifs.

779
01:04:34.137 --> 01:04:37.744
Quiet! Don't raise your voice!

780
01:04:37.907 --> 01:04:41.980
Oh, I will talk as loud as I want.

781
01:04:42.145 --> 01:04:45.854
No, you won't!
Or I'll have you whacked!

782
01:04:47.550 --> 01:04:48.551
What?

783
01:04:48.718 --> 01:04:51.858
If you tell anyone,
I'll have you taken out!

784
01:04:52.021 --> 01:04:56.231
I know people. Remember Banducci's
cousin? The liquor wholesaler?

785
01:04:57.527 --> 01:05:00.030
Are you threatening me?

786
01:05:00.196 --> 01:05:02.904
Fine, kill me!

787
01:05:03.066 --> 01:05:05.672
My God, I have kept
our secret for years.

788
01:05:05.835 --> 01:05:07.610
Never once have I...

789
01:05:09.539 --> 01:05:11.246
Do you know how hard
this has been for me?

790
01:05:13.543 --> 01:05:15.216
I don't have any friends.

791
01:05:15.378 --> 01:05:18.552
I've lied to my own child.

792
01:05:22.051 --> 01:05:25.965
Life imitating art...
a crying Keane!

793
01:05:27.290 --> 01:05:29.270
Walter, what do you want?

794
01:05:31.761 --> 01:05:34.469
Everything with you is calculated.

795
01:05:34.631 --> 01:05:38.044
We're back where we had our first date.

796
01:05:42.305 --> 01:05:46.776
Look, I don't deny I need you.
You're the one with the gift.

797
01:05:47.911 --> 01:05:49.322
Now, there's a shot...

798
01:05:49.479 --> 01:05:53.393
Oh, God, I'm shaking I'm so excited.

799
01:05:53.550 --> 01:05:56.121
The New York World's Fair.

800
01:05:56.286 --> 01:06:00.757
Seventy million visitors.
Opening day, I unveil my masterpiece!

801
01:06:00.957 --> 01:06:04.598
- What masterpiece?
- Exactly!

802
01:06:04.828 --> 01:06:06.967
What have we been missing all this time?

803
01:06:07.130 --> 01:06:10.668
Da Vinci has his Mona Lisa,
Renoir has his Boatmen's Lunch.

804
01:06:10.834 --> 01:06:13.110
Where's my defining statement?

805
01:06:13.269 --> 01:06:15.249
You sound insane.

806
01:06:15.438 --> 01:06:18.612
An artist doesn't
just announce his masterpiece.

807
01:06:18.775 --> 01:06:22.154
Why not? Didn't Michelangelo
know he was hitting a home run

808
01:06:22.312 --> 01:06:24.352
when he was on his back
painting the Sistine Chapel?

809
01:06:24.514 --> 01:06:26.960
That took him four years.

810
01:06:27.116 --> 01:06:29.596
But here's the best part.

811
01:06:29.752 --> 01:06:31.129
It's for UNICEF.

812
01:06:32.188 --> 01:06:34.759
UNICEF sponsors the Hall of Education.

813
01:06:34.924 --> 01:06:39.373
We can finally give back
to the children of the world.

814
01:06:44.133 --> 01:06:45.373
<i>Hello, everybody.</i>

815
01:06:45.535 --> 01:06:50.280
<i>I'm Lowell Thomas, standing in front
of what will soon be</i>

816
01:06:50.440 --> 01:06:53.148
<i>one of the wonders of the modern world.</i>

817
01:07:08.725 --> 01:07:11.535
<i>When people ask me,
'Why did she stay?</i>

818
01:07:11.694 --> 01:07:14.038
<i>Was ii fear? Lack of confidence?“</i>

819
01:07:14.364 --> 01:07:17.311
<i>Margaret was trapped
in</i> a <i>lie that she'd helped create.</i>

820
01:07:17.467 --> 01:07:19.674
<i>And now the cover-up
was worse than the crime.</i>

821
01:07:22.071 --> 01:07:25.917
Why did you have
to promise them Cinerama size?

822
01:07:26.075 --> 01:07:29.386
Because it has to encompass
all children, all races.

823
01:07:29.546 --> 01:07:33.926
Hundreds of stricken faces,
marching to infinity.

824
01:07:34.083 --> 01:07:36.427
The ultimate Walter Keane.

825
01:07:36.586 --> 01:07:40.329
A publisher says ifs good timing
to put out a coffee table book.

826
01:07:40.490 --> 01:07:44.370
<i>You know, classy, Tomorrow's Masters.</i>

827
01:07:44.527 --> 01:07:48.168
They need my early portfolio,
my artistic evolution.

828
01:07:48.331 --> 01:07:52.040
Yes, where are all
of your preliminary sketches? Hm'?

829
01:07:52.201 --> 01:07:54.272
All of that time
that you spent in art school,

830
01:07:54.437 --> 01:07:57.281
and we seem to have waylaid
all of your youthful experiments.

831
01:07:57.440 --> 01:08:01.286
All of those unfinished charcoals.
They've gotta be around here somewhere.

832
01:08:04.113 --> 01:08:07.788
I know you're being sarcastic.
These are good ideas.

833
01:08:07.951 --> 01:08:11.763
Berlin war orphans,
early self-portraits.

834
01:08:12.088 --> 01:08:14.762
Get outta here. I'm trying to work.

835
01:08:18.094 --> 01:08:21.871
Mom! What about dinner'?

836
01:08:23.266 --> 01:08:25.143
Mom, are you home'?

837
01:08:30.239 --> 01:08:31.411
Mom?

838
01:09:15.084 --> 01:09:16.825
Mom.

839
01:09:20.490 --> 01:09:23.494
Jane, this is Walter's studio!
You need to leave!

840
01:09:23.793 --> 01:09:25.329
Mom...

841
01:09:26.663 --> 01:09:28.438
I know.

842
01:09:29.465 --> 01:09:31.604
No, you don't know anything.

843
01:09:31.768 --> 01:09:34.180
I'm not a child anymore.

844
01:09:36.673 --> 01:09:37.674
Jane.

845
01:09:38.941 --> 01:09:40.011
Jane!

846
01:09:55.892 --> 01:09:58.736
Five... six pages?

847
01:09:58.895 --> 01:10:02.900
Is there something here I'm missing?
He's like the Hula Hoop.

848
01:10:03.066 --> 01:10:05.808
He just won't go away.

849
01:10:06.002 --> 01:10:10.348
"Will be unveiled in the Grand Pavilion
of the Hall of Education.

850
01:10:10.506 --> 01:10:14.648
Internationally celebrated artist
has been selected.

851
01:10:14.811 --> 01:10:18.258
Will represent the aspirations
of children worldwide."

852
01:10:18.414 --> 01:10:20.052
This is absurd!

853
01:10:28.991 --> 01:10:32.029
And who was on the selection committee?

854
01:10:32.195 --> 01:10:35.608
Oh, well, there wasn't a committee,
per se.

855
01:10:35.832 --> 01:10:39.439
We just had a luncheon
with me and Ed and Jerome

856
01:10:39.602 --> 01:10:40.876
and Jerome's wife.

857
01:10:41.037 --> 01:10:44.246
Though, technically,
we didn't invite submissions.

858
01:10:44.407 --> 01:10:47.081
Mr. Keane just contacted us directly.

859
01:10:53.082 --> 01:10:55.062
Come on.

860
01:10:55.218 --> 01:10:59.758
Wednesday the World's Fair opens,
Thursday our book goes on sale.

861
01:11:00.089 --> 01:11:02.228
Friday I file for divorce.

862
01:11:02.391 --> 01:11:05.463
Oh, why are you always so miserable?

863
01:11:07.964 --> 01:11:12.242
Well, I'm going to enjoy
my afternoon.

864
01:11:14.804 --> 01:11:18.308
Mrs. Teasdale, Walter Keane.

865
01:11:18.474 --> 01:11:23.480
I just wanted to thank you for hosting
this absolutely enchanting soiree.

866
01:11:23.646 --> 01:11:25.990
Well, you are most welcome.

867
01:11:28.417 --> 01:11:30.920
Hey, Keane.
Have you seen The Times?

868
01:11:31.087 --> 01:11:36.127
Honestly, I've been so busy all day
preparing for this lovely occasion.

869
01:11:36.292 --> 01:11:39.501
I think you should read The Times.

870
01:12:05.955 --> 01:12:08.333
How could anybody
say something so cruel'?

871
01:12:08.491 --> 01:12:12.962
What do you care? It's my name
being dragged through the mud!

872
01:12:13.129 --> 01:12:15.871
- Is he here?
- Uh... yes.

873
01:12:16.032 --> 01:12:19.275
Which is perhaps why it's best
for everybody if you...

874
01:12:23.940 --> 01:12:25.681
Who wrote this shit!

875
01:12:28.644 --> 01:12:32.387
Mr. Keane, this is not the venue.

876
01:12:32.548 --> 01:12:35.188
Perhaps you'd like to write a letter
to the editor.

877
01:12:35.351 --> 01:12:37.353
What are you afraid of!

878
01:12:37.520 --> 01:12:41.468
Just because people like my work,
that means automatically it's bad?

879
01:12:41.624 --> 01:12:44.400
No, but it doesn't make it art either.

880
01:12:44.560 --> 01:12:46.801
Art should elevate, not pander.

881
01:12:46.963 --> 01:12:49.034
Particularly in a Hall of Education.

882
01:12:49.198 --> 01:12:52.509
You have no idea!
Why does someone become a critic?

883
01:12:52.668 --> 01:12:56.878
- Because he cannot create!
- Oh, dear. That moldy chestnut.

884
01:12:57.039 --> 01:12:58.040
Don't interrupt!

885
01:12:58.207 --> 01:13:01.654
You don't know what it's like
to put your emotions out there,

886
01:13:01.811 --> 01:13:04.655
naked for the whole world to see!

887
01:13:04.814 --> 01:13:07.522
What emotions?
It's synthetic hack work.

888
01:13:07.683 --> 01:13:10.323
Your masterpiece has an infinity
of Keanes,

889
01:13:10.486 --> 01:13:13.262
which makes it an infinity of kitsch.

890
01:14:00.236 --> 01:14:03.115
What is wrong with
the lowest common denominator'?

891
01:14:04.106 --> 01:14:05.483
Huh?

892
01:14:06.442 --> 01:14:09.150
That's what this country was built on!

893
01:14:14.083 --> 01:14:16.154
I'm gonna sue everybody.

894
01:14:17.553 --> 01:14:19.726
I'm gonna sue everybody.

895
01:14:19.889 --> 01:14:21.493
Everybody.

896
01:14:25.461 --> 01:14:29.466
I'm gonna sue this pansy critic.

897
01:14:29.632 --> 01:14:32.340
And sue the World's Fair.

898
01:14:32.501 --> 01:14:35.004
And I'm gonna sue UNICEF.

899
01:14:39.175 --> 01:14:43.646
I'm gonna take down UNICEF,

900
01:14:43.813 --> 01:14:47.659
and all their precious little
boxes of dimes.

901
01:14:49.318 --> 01:14:50.319
Ah!

902
01:14:58.761 --> 01:15:01.173
But I can't sue you, can I'?

903
01:15:03.933 --> 01:15:07.176
You are the ultimate betrayal.

904
01:15:07.336 --> 01:15:10.977
You failed me with that painting!

905
01:15:18.547 --> 01:15:24.156
You crossed over from sentimentality
to kitsch.

906
01:15:24.320 --> 01:15:26.459
Ah! Walter, stop it!

907
01:15:38.834 --> 01:15:41.542
You like making me look bad?

908
01:15:41.704 --> 01:15:43.809
You enjoy people laughing at me?

909
01:15:45.808 --> 01:15:48.254
Open up! Open UP!

910
01:15:48.411 --> 01:15:51.221
Just let me in!

911
01:16:07.196 --> 01:16:09.733
I can see you!

912
01:16:20.876 --> 01:16:24.619
I'm gonna burn you out.

913
01:16:27.016 --> 01:16:30.930
You're gonna blow up
like an atom bomb.

914
01:16:37.860 --> 01:16:39.066
Come on!

915
01:16:45.901 --> 01:16:46.902
Where are you'?

916
01:16:59.648 --> 01:17:03.755
I am so sorry that I was not
the mother I could have been.

917
01:17:05.121 --> 01:17:07.965
I should have done this years ago.

918
01:17:08.124 --> 01:17:11.298
Where are we going?
We don't even have any clothes.

919
01:17:11.460 --> 01:17:14.771
Uh... where we're going,
we don't need much.

920
01:17:17.066 --> 01:17:18.170
Hawaii.

921
01:17:20.169 --> 01:17:22.080
- Really?
- Yeah.

922
01:17:22.238 --> 01:17:24.980
Yes, Hawaii,
because it's paradise there.

923
01:17:25.141 --> 01:17:28.850
There's... birds and ﬂowers
and beautiful colors,

924
01:17:29.011 --> 01:17:33.187
and... we're gonna make a new life
for ourselves.

925
01:17:34.683 --> 01:17:37.527
Okay? That's my girl.

926
01:18:30.506 --> 01:18:33.749
- Hello?
- Boy, you were sure hard to track down.

927
01:18:33.909 --> 01:18:35.388
Thought I might never find you.

928
01:18:37.346 --> 01:18:38.484
Walter...

929
01:18:38.647 --> 01:18:41.093
I'm a little agitated.

930
01:18:41.350 --> 01:18:45.264
I got the strangest papers
in the mail today.

931
01:18:45.487 --> 01:18:49.731
It's a decree of legal separation
and I'd appreciate it if you'd sign it.

932
01:18:49.892 --> 01:18:53.772
Yeah. Aren't you acting a little rash?

933
01:18:53.996 --> 01:18:57.000
Walter, I want a divorce.

934
01:18:57.166 --> 01:18:59.168
<i>It hurts to hear you
say these words.</i>

935
01:18:59.335 --> 01:19:01.906
I sure hate that it's come to this.

936
01:19:02.071 --> 01:19:06.451
Well, I suppose I could agree
to a split,

937
01:19:06.609 --> 01:19:11.683
as long as... as you assign me all the
rights to every painting ever produced.

938
01:19:15.884 --> 01:19:18.262
- If that's the price.
- Really?

939
01:19:20.389 --> 01:19:22.426
<i>OKAY-</i>

940
01:19:22.591 --> 01:19:25.936
Well, then we have to consider
future revenue streams.

941
01:19:26.095 --> 01:19:28.701
My God, Walter,
how much more money do you need?

942
01:19:28.864 --> 01:19:31.401
If you want me out of your life,
here's my terms.

943
01:19:31.567 --> 01:19:36.539
You'll have to paint me 100 more waifs,
100 more Walter Keanes.

944
01:19:39.708 --> 01:19:42.882
Jane?

945
01:19:44.246 --> 01:19:46.283
I'm gonna go to the post office.
You wanna come?

946
01:19:46.448 --> 01:19:48.758
No, I'm gonna go surf with the gang.

947
01:19:48.917 --> 01:19:51.056
Jane, I think that your friends
are a little wild.

948
01:19:51.220 --> 01:19:54.099
Loosen up, Mom. You're impossible.

949
01:19:54.256 --> 01:19:57.897
You move me all the way to Hawaii, and
then when I actually make some friends,

950
01:19:58.093 --> 01:20:00.095
and all you do is complain about them.

951
01:20:00.262 --> 01:20:02.674
Maybe you need to make some.

952
01:20:02.831 --> 01:20:06.074
You know that I cannot have people
over to the house.

953
01:20:06.235 --> 01:20:08.943
That's right, or else they'd see
the precious paintings.

954
01:20:49.878 --> 01:20:54.486
Hello. We're visiting everyone in this
neighborhood with an important message.

955
01:20:54.650 --> 01:20:57.654
We have something to share with you
about the wonderful things

956
01:20:57.820 --> 01:21:00.733
that Gods kingdom will do for mankind.

957
01:21:00.889 --> 01:21:03.460
Well, from where I'm standing,
I don't see much good anywhere.

958
01:21:03.625 --> 01:21:06.799
Just a lot of pride and thievery
and people treating each other poorly.

959
01:21:06.962 --> 01:21:09.499
Do you know what it says
in Timothy 311-5?

960
01:21:10.466 --> 01:21:15.313
"In the last days, critical times
hard lo deal with will be here.

961
01:21:15.738 --> 01:21:18.480
For men will be lovers of themselves."

962
01:21:18.640 --> 01:21:19.812
Sounds like my ex-husband.

963
01:21:28.984 --> 01:21:31.897
Um... would you like to come in?

964
01:21:37.326 --> 01:21:41.069
It says right here,
"A worshipper of Jehovah

965
01:21:41.230 --> 01:21:43.005
must be honest in all things."

966
01:21:44.566 --> 01:21:47.376
I just can't believe you
let people in the house.

967
01:21:47.903 --> 01:21:49.883
It also says,

968
01:21:50.038 --> 01:21:55.181
"No lies speak the truth.
Let the stealer steal no more."

969
01:22:00.382 --> 01:22:03.955
Shit, this is crazy, man.
All these copies.

970
01:22:04.119 --> 01:22:06.759
- You're like Warhol.
- No.

971
01:22:06.922 --> 01:22:10.802
Warhol's like me.
That fruit ﬂy stole my act.

972
01:22:10.959 --> 01:22:13.940
Ah! My art supplies.

973
01:22:14.096 --> 01:22:19.273
The Factory. I had a factory before
he even knew what a soup can was.

974
01:22:35.284 --> 01:22:37.525
<i>Oh, yeah.
We got</i> a <i>special guest today.</i>

975
01:22:37.619 --> 01:22:40.759
<i>A world-famous celebrity.</i>
So <i>let's give</i> a <i>big "aloha"</i>

976
01:22:40.923 --> 01:22:42.960
to Margaret Keane.

977
01:22:43.125 --> 01:22:46.231
So is it true your husband, Walter,

978
01:22:46.395 --> 01:22:48.773
is the top-selling painter in the world?

979
01:22:52.734 --> 01:22:54.839
No, Big Lolo.

980
01:22:55.003 --> 01:22:58.007
Everything you just said is false.

981
01:22:59.074 --> 01:23:02.647
One, Walter and I are no longer married.

982
01:23:02.811 --> 01:23:06.418
And two, he...

983
01:23:06.582 --> 01:23:08.994
is not a painter.

984
01:23:11.687 --> 01:23:16.636
But... am I mixed up? Ain't he
the guy who does the crazy eyes?

985
01:23:17.826 --> 01:23:19.806
No.

986
01:23:19.962 --> 01:23:23.136
Although he has been taking credit
for it for ten years.

987
01:23:24.867 --> 01:23:29.509
I am the only painter in the family.

988
01:23:51.693 --> 01:23:52.728
Whoo!

989
01:24:07.643 --> 01:24:09.850
You've got to be kidding.

990
01:24:10.012 --> 01:24:11.650
<i>You know what pissed me off?</i>

991
01:24:11.813 --> 01:24:14.885
<i>Margaret gave the scoop to some
button-pushing Big Lolo...</i>

992
01:24:15.050 --> 01:24:16.051
Jesus!

993
01:24:16.218 --> 01:24:18.698
<i>Instead of</i> a <i>respected
journalist, like myself.</i>

994
01:24:18.854 --> 01:24:22.028
<i>I</i> knew n.

995
01:24:28.096 --> 01:24:29.871
Who would want credit?

996
01:24:39.074 --> 01:24:41.714
Margaret's gone berserk.
You gotta help me.

997
01:24:41.877 --> 01:24:43.254
I need a story.

998
01:24:43.412 --> 01:24:46.859
Wire story, national.
Just to calm things down.

999
01:24:47.015 --> 01:24:50.189
I don't know, Walter.
What she said is pretty inﬂammatory.

1000
01:24:50.352 --> 01:24:53.458
But it's nuts!
Doesn't even make sense.

1001
01:24:53.622 --> 01:24:56.398
I mean, I studied art
at the Beaux-Arts in Paris.

1002
01:24:56.558 --> 01:24:59.300
She was still a little kid
in Tennessee.

1003
01:24:59.461 --> 01:25:00.735
LOOK.

1004
01:25:02.864 --> 01:25:04.741
My early sketchings.

1005
01:25:07.202 --> 01:25:10.615
Berlin orphans, 1949.

1006
01:25:11.573 --> 01:25:13.280
Why would Margaret
do something like this?

1007
01:25:13.442 --> 01:25:15.786
She's unhinged.

1008
01:25:15.944 --> 01:25:17.946
She left me to move into the jungle.

1009
01:25:18.113 --> 01:25:20.719
Fell in with religious zealots!

1010
01:25:23.218 --> 01:25:25.164
Jehovah's Witnesses.

1011
01:25:25.320 --> 01:25:28.824
I really don't know all that much
about them.

1012
01:25:28.991 --> 01:25:31.494
They don't celebrate Christmas,
can't salute the flag.

1013
01:25:31.660 --> 01:25:34.140
They won't even let Janie go
to the prom!

1014
01:25:34.296 --> 01:25:35.673
Oh...

1015
01:25:37.466 --> 01:25:39.241
He makes me sound crazy.

1016
01:25:39.401 --> 01:25:42.610
He said that I copied him,
that he taught me how to paint.

1017
01:25:42.771 --> 01:25:47.117
"She used a slide projector to
trace my work and fill in the colors."

1018
01:25:47.275 --> 01:25:50.779
- Which part of that isn't true?
- All of it.

1019
01:25:50.946 --> 01:25:53.517
I feel good about myself
for the first time in years,

1020
01:25:53.682 --> 01:25:56.492
and I can't let Walter
just take that away from me.

1021
01:25:57.919 --> 01:26:00.456
Hey, is Jehovah okay with suing?

1022
01:26:04.426 --> 01:26:08.135
Seventeen million dollars.
The an world is abuzz.

1023
01:26:08.296 --> 01:26:10.833
Today, at federal court, lawyers will
present their opening arguments

1024
01:26:10.999 --> 01:26:14.879
in the case of Margaret Keane vs.
Walter Keane and Gannett Newspapers,

1025
01:26:15.037 --> 01:26:18.041
a trial that could produce
the largest libel and slander reward

1026
01:26:18.206 --> 01:26:20.379
- in Hawaiian history.
- Step aside, please.

1027
01:26:33.488 --> 01:26:34.694
- Mr. Keane.
- Yes?

1028
01:26:34.856 --> 01:26:36.130
Are you at all concerned
about the charges'?

1029
01:26:36.291 --> 01:26:37.827
I'm angry as hell.

1030
01:26:37.993 --> 01:26:41.304
But I'm lucky I have the mighty
Gannett News company watching my back.

1031
01:26:41.463 --> 01:26:43.409
I expect this whole trial
dismissed by noon.

1032
01:26:43.565 --> 01:26:47.638
My only concern is that somebody
get this woman some psychiatric help.

1033
01:26:47.803 --> 01:26:49.874
- She needs it.
- All right. Excuse me, gentlemen.

1034
01:26:50.038 --> 01:26:51.312
Excuse me. Excuse me, please.

1035
01:26:55.077 --> 01:26:58.388
Margaret Keane is a public figure,
and as such, she has to prove

1036
01:26:58.547 --> 01:27:02.518
that our newspapers published statements
aware of probable falsity.

1037
01:27:02.684 --> 01:27:05.392
New there's no evidence to suggest
that our editors could have known

1038
01:27:05.554 --> 01:27:06.862
these assertions were untrue.

1039
01:27:07.022 --> 01:27:12.938
We would like to submit 692 articles
and interviews in which Mrs. Keane

1040
01:27:13.095 --> 01:27:17.737
credits Mr. Keane as the painter
of the so-called big-eye children.

1041
01:27:27.909 --> 01:27:30.082
How many years back do these go?

1042
01:27:30.245 --> 01:27:33.226
Mrs. Keane has been making
these statements since 1958.

1043
01:27:35.283 --> 01:27:37.422
This is a very strange case.

1044
01:27:38.386 --> 01:27:41.196
These paintings hang in museums
all over the world,

1045
01:27:41.356 --> 01:27:43.131
attributed to Mr. Keane.

1046
01:27:44.025 --> 01:27:47.097
And regardless of the truth,
Mrs. Keane has contributed

1047
01:27:47.262 --> 01:27:49.242
immeasurably to the confusion.

1048
01:27:51.133 --> 01:27:52.694
Seems impossible that Gannett's actions

1049
01:27:52.801 --> 01:27:55.441
would ever meet the legal standards
for libel.

1050
01:27:55.604 --> 01:27:57.641
So the charge against them is dismissed.

1051
01:27:57.806 --> 01:27:58.944
Thank you, Your Honor.

1052
01:28:02.978 --> 01:28:06.926
- Good luck, Keane.
- Good luck? Where are you going?

1053
01:28:07.082 --> 01:28:10.393
We were charged with libel.
You are charged with slander.

1054
01:28:19.795 --> 01:28:21.797
Mr. Keane?

1055
01:28:21.963 --> 01:28:24.739
You appear to be without counsel.

1056
01:28:24.900 --> 01:28:27.972
Would you like a postponement
in order to get your affairs in order?

1057
01:28:29.805 --> 01:28:31.284
I've always taken care of myself.

1058
01:28:31.439 --> 01:28:34.613
I don't need a bunch of rent-a-suits
to defend my good name.

1059
01:28:34.776 --> 01:28:36.449
Let's proceed.

1060
01:28:49.791 --> 01:28:52.829
<i>I'm concerned about my old pal,
Walter Keane.</i>

1061
01:28:52.994 --> 01:28:55.634
<i>The Hawaiian heat
may have cooked his brain.</i>

1062
01:28:55.797 --> 01:28:58.277
<i>The only thing he knows
about courtrooms and Sawyers</i>

1063
01:28:58.433 --> 01:29:01.505
<i>comes from watching Perry Mason
on television.</i>

1064
01:29:01.670 --> 01:29:04.981
I am the sole creator of my art.

1065
01:29:06.708 --> 01:29:08.312
This is my entire life.

1066
01:29:08.476 --> 01:29:10.979
My contribution to the world.

1067
01:29:11.146 --> 01:29:15.117
Mr. Keane, I've told you, you must
ask the witness questions.

1068
01:29:15.784 --> 01:29:18.905
If you're acting as your own attorney,
you cannot make statements at this time.

1069
01:29:18.987 --> 01:29:20.022
Yeah. All right.

1070
01:29:21.656 --> 01:29:24.330
It's hard to keep
all these things straight.

1071
01:29:25.794 --> 01:29:31.301
Mrs. Keane, how do you expect anybody
to swallow your fantastic story?

1072
01:29:31.466 --> 01:29:32.672
Mr. Keane...

1073
01:29:38.874 --> 01:29:43.084
Mrs. Keane, you seem to be a lucid,

1074
01:29:43.345 --> 01:29:46.258
reasonably intelligent woman.

1075
01:29:46.481 --> 01:29:52.193
So how could you possibly have gone
along with a far-out scheme like that?

1076
01:29:54.723 --> 01:29:57.033
I felt forced into it.

1077
01:29:58.326 --> 01:29:59.703
You had me, um...

1078
01:30:03.331 --> 01:30:04.742
He had me dominated.

1079
01:30:06.234 --> 01:30:09.704
He would rant and rave if I didn't
give him what he wanted.

1080
01:30:09.871 --> 01:30:13.944
I was afraid and I didn't see
any option, so I went along.

1081
01:30:14.109 --> 01:30:15.713
May I remind you that you're under oath?

1082
01:30:19.915 --> 01:30:21.519
I just gave in.

1083
01:30:21.683 --> 01:30:24.289
I allowed him

1084
01:30:24.452 --> 01:30:26.762
to take credit for the big eyes.

1085
01:30:26.922 --> 01:30:29.425
They were a reﬂection of my feelings,

1086
01:30:29.624 --> 01:30:32.730
and it was like losing a child.

1087
01:30:32.894 --> 01:30:34.601
But I was weak.

1088
01:30:34.930 --> 01:30:40.608
I didn't think that I could leave
and support myself and my daughter.

1089
01:30:40.769 --> 01:30:45.878
And he said that nobody would buy
the paintings without his personality.

1090
01:30:47.409 --> 01:30:49.411
Maybe he was right.

1091
01:30:50.779 --> 01:30:54.955
You are... very talented
at being charming.

1092
01:30:56.451 --> 01:31:00.422
And you are a genius
at sales and promotion.

1093
01:31:00.588 --> 01:31:05.662
Hm! It sounds like you've
described two different men.

1094
01:31:05.827 --> 01:31:06.931
Hmm.

1095
01:31:07.095 --> 01:31:09.575
One a sadistic ogre,

1096
01:31:09.731 --> 01:31:13.474
and the other one
a delightful ban vivant.

1097
01:31:13.635 --> 01:31:16.479
Well, that's you, Walter.
You're Jekyll and Hyde.

1098
01:31:16.638 --> 01:31:19.278
That's an outrageous statement!

1099
01:31:19.441 --> 01:31:21.921
- I demand we strike it off the record!
- Overruled!

1100
01:31:22.077 --> 01:31:24.580
No, you were outrageous,

1101
01:31:24.746 --> 01:31:26.953
constantly criticizing me,
wearing me down,

1102
01:31:27.115 --> 01:31:30.460
saying that you would have me
knocked off if I told the truth.

1103
01:31:30.618 --> 01:31:32.495
- Well, I am telling the truth.
- Your Honor!

1104
01:31:33.355 --> 01:31:36.359
- I ask for mistrial.
- You're a liar!

1105
01:31:36.524 --> 01:31:38.629
You don't even know what the truth is!

1106
01:31:38.793 --> 01:31:42.832
You don't even know I'm saying the truth
now, and you cannot shut me up!

1107
01:31:42.998 --> 01:31:46.969
Hey, hey, hey!
This is not a domestic squabble!

1108
01:31:47.135 --> 01:31:48.375
Or maybe it is.

1109
01:31:48.536 --> 01:31:52.609
But the rest of us have no interest
in watching you two go at it.

1110
01:31:55.810 --> 01:31:58.814
I'm sorry for my emotion.

1111
01:32:00.148 --> 01:32:01.718
I'm an artist.

1112
01:32:03.518 --> 01:32:04.792
Maybe.

1113
01:32:06.521 --> 01:32:09.900
Your Honor, I call <i>as</i> my witness

1114
01:32:10.058 --> 01:32:14.063
Mr. Walter Stanley Keane.

1115
01:32:20.201 --> 01:32:21.805
Do you swear to tell the truth,
the whole truth,

1116
01:32:21.970 --> 01:32:22.971
- and nothing but the truth...
- Yes.

1117
01:32:23.138 --> 01:32:25.482
- ...so help you God?
- Yes.

1118
01:32:33.581 --> 01:32:37.723
Mr. Keane, there seems
to be a lot of innuendo

1119
01:32:37.886 --> 01:32:42.858
and contradictory testimony
about the genesis of the big-eyed waifs.

1120
01:32:43.024 --> 01:32:46.597
Would you mind clarifying
to this court

1121
01:32:46.761 --> 01:32:49.674
who spawned these paintings?

1122
01:32:54.202 --> 01:32:56.910
Why, I created these children.

1123
01:33:01.276 --> 01:33:04.086
The choreography is not necessary.
Just sit down and testify.

1124
01:33:12.887 --> 01:33:15.390
I had a wonderful life.

1125
01:33:15.557 --> 01:33:17.400
I was an artist,

1126
01:33:17.559 --> 01:33:20.802
a friend of untold celebrities.

1127
01:33:20.962 --> 01:33:23.943
Yet, when I look back at it all,

1128
01:33:24.466 --> 01:33:26.969
to define what really mattered,

1129
01:33:27.135 --> 01:33:31.811
it was that I was dedicated
to the hungry children of the world.

1130
01:33:34.709 --> 01:33:37.588
It all began in... in Berlin.

1131
01:33:37.745 --> 01:33:39.747
After World War ll.

1132
01:33:39.914 --> 01:33:42.326
The orphans...

1133
01:33:42.484 --> 01:33:44.828
clutching the barbed wire.

1134
01:33:44.986 --> 01:33:48.991
Their bodies lacerated.

1135
01:33:49.157 --> 01:33:51.159
Their fingers scrawny,

1136
01:33:51.326 --> 01:33:53.636
their eyes big.

1137
01:33:55.230 --> 01:33:59.838
- Miss Joan Crawford walked up...
- Mr. Keane. Sit down.

1138
01:34:00.001 --> 01:34:01.947
Marilyn Monroe...

1139
01:34:02.103 --> 01:34:03.912
Wayne Newton and I...

1140
01:34:04.072 --> 01:34:06.678
Jerry Lewis calls me and says,
"I want you to paint me

1141
01:34:06.841 --> 01:34:08.445
and my family in clown suits."

1142
01:34:08.610 --> 01:34:13.116
Miss Natalie Wood walked up to me
and said,

1143
01:34:13.281 --> 01:34:16.956
"This is the greatest single painting

1144
01:34:17.118 --> 01:34:19.655
I've ever seen in my entire life!"

1145
01:34:19.821 --> 01:34:21.858
- You're done!
- I'm not finished.

1146
01:34:22.023 --> 01:34:23.161
Actually, you are.

1147
01:34:23.324 --> 01:34:26.203
I cannot stomach one more wild tangent
or shaggy dog tale.

1148
01:34:26.361 --> 01:34:28.307
You're not testifying,
you're filibustering.

1149
01:34:28.463 --> 01:34:29.983
Federal courts are overburdened enough

1150
01:34:30.098 --> 01:34:32.135
without all your
docket-clogging nonsense.

1151
01:34:32.300 --> 01:34:35.179
We can stay here
until we grow old and die,

1152
01:34:35.336 --> 01:34:36.976
but it's obvious that
this case boils down

1153
01:34:37.105 --> 01:34:39.483
to your word versus Mrs. Keane's word.

1154
01:34:39.641 --> 01:34:42.815
- Mistrial.
- No, it's not a mistrial!

1155
01:34:44.012 --> 01:34:48.518
In my opinion, there's only one way
to clear this thing up.

1156
01:34:49.784 --> 01:34:51.786
You're both going to paint.

1157
01:34:54.489 --> 01:34:58.631
All right, bring those easels down,
set them up on both sides of the bench.

1158
01:35:11.306 --> 01:35:15.220
Now, I'm not looking for a masterpiece.
I don't know much about these things.

1159
01:35:15.376 --> 01:35:19.324
I'm a jurist, not an art critic.
But is one hour enough?

1160
01:35:19.480 --> 01:35:21.153
Yes, Your Honor.

1161
01:35:24.752 --> 01:35:29.223
Okay, then. You've both been provided
with identical supplies.

1162
01:35:29.390 --> 01:35:33.930
So, without any further business,
Mrs. Keane, Mr. Keane, the court is yours.

1163
01:36:40.995 --> 01:36:42.235
Mr. Keane?

1164
01:36:45.500 --> 01:36:47.002
I'm just setting the mood.

1165
01:36:48.503 --> 01:36:51.450
Waiting for the muse to strike.

1166
01:36:52.507 --> 01:36:55.113
Well, your muse has 58 minutes.

1167
01:37:23.471 --> 01:37:24.848
Ah!

1168
01:37:25.006 --> 01:37:27.145
Mr. Keane? Are you all right?

1169
01:37:27.308 --> 01:37:29.652
Oh, shit.

1170
01:37:29.811 --> 01:37:32.985
My old shoulder injury
just flared up.

1171
01:37:33.147 --> 01:37:35.889
I have, um, um... bad muscles.

1172
01:37:36.050 --> 01:37:40.521
It's... I've been taking medication
for the inflammation.

1173
01:37:41.322 --> 01:37:42.858
So...

1174
01:37:56.804 --> 01:37:58.909
I don't think I'll
be able to paint today.

1175
01:38:17.325 --> 01:38:19.999
The jury found in favor
of Margaret Keane on all points.

1176
01:38:20.161 --> 01:38:23.199
She won on charges of defamation,
emotional distress,

1177
01:38:23.364 --> 01:38:25.002
and damaged reputation.

1178
01:38:25.166 --> 01:38:27.043
- Congratulations!
- Congratulations, Margaret!

1179
01:38:27.201 --> 01:38:30.842
- Thank you, thank you very much.
- Congratulations!

1180
01:38:31.005 --> 01:38:32.965
Margaret, what are you going
to call the painting?

1181
01:38:33.408 --> 01:38:37.857
Uh... Exhibit 224.

1182
01:38:39.013 --> 01:38:42.051
Margaret, can I possibly
have your autograph?

1183
01:38:43.017 --> 01:38:47.056
<i>her daughter and her paintings.</i>

1184
01:38:47.221 --> 01:38:49.929
<i>And after all the crazy turns
the story took</i>

1185
01:38:50.091 --> 01:38:53.038
<i>she came out at the end
with both of them.</i>
